As we shall see, the Voronoi cells of ordinary materials can be unexpectedly complex and further insight is needed to make the problem conceptually and numerically tractable. As a working example, we chose here to showcase silicon in its both crystalline and liquid phases. 
The crystalline structure at $0$K is shown in Fig.~\ref{Fig:SiOverview}, together with the Voronoi cells of the two atoms in the repeating Bravais lattice cells. We recall that a single Voronoi cell at 0K tiles the entire $\RM^3$ space, when acted on by the elements of the space symmetry group $Fd\bar 3 m$ of the silicon crystal \cite{Singh93,wondratschek2004international}. Thus, at finite temperatures, apart from thermal fluctuations, all Voronoi cells are translations and rotations of one fixed geometry. This simplifies the analysis, but does not enable it: The conclusions of this section apply to any crystalline material.

