%%%%\subsection{Check that all geometry works fine}
%%%%Let $G$ be an affine algebraic group over a field $\genfd$. Let $\mathcal{L}(G)$ be its "tangent space at the unit", which consists of differentiations of $\OO(G)$ \cite{Hochschild}. By definition, these are $\genfd$-linear functionals $X_e$ on $\OO(G)$ with the property $\langle X_e, fg \rangle  = \epsilon(f)\langle X_e,g\rangle + \langle X_e,f\rangle \epsilon(g)$. Denote by $\mathcal{D}(G)$ the Lie algebra of derivations of  $\OO(G)$, that is $\genfd$-linear maps $D \colon \OO(G) \to \OO(G)$ such that $D(fg) = D(f)g + f D(g)$. Let $(X_1)_e,\ldots,(X_n)_e$ be the basis of $\mathcal{L}(G)$. Define derivations $X_i \in \mathcal{D}(G)$ of $\OO(G)$ by $X_i(f) = f_{(1)} \langle (X_i)_e, f_{(2)} \rangle$. Then every $X_i$ is left invariant derivation, that is, $X(f\circ L_g) = X(f) \circ L_g$. Proof. Define also derivations $Y_i$ of $\OO(G)$ by $Y_i(f) = \langle (X_i)_e, f_{(1)} \rangle  f_{(2)}$. Then every $Y_i$ is a right invariant derivation, that is, $Y_i(f\circ R_g) = Y_i(f) \circ R_g$. Proof. By Theorem III.3.1. in \cite{Hochschild}, $\OO(G) \otimes \mathcal{L}(G) \cong \mathcal{D}(G)$, by mapping rule $g \otimes (X_i)_e \mapsto (f \mapsto gf_{(1)} \langle (X_i)_e, f_{(2)} \rangle)$. Therefore, there exist functions $\bar\OO^i_j \in \OO(G)$ such that $Y_j = \bar\OO^i_j X_i$. Prove that the matrix $\OO$ is invertible and its properties. Denote by $\gL$ the image of $\genfd \otimes \mathcal{L}(G)$ in $\mathcal D(G)$, that is, the $\genfd$-vector subspace spanned by $X_1,\ldots, X_n$. This is a Lie subalgebra of $\mathcal{D}(G)$. Proof. Denote by $U(\gL)$ the universal enveloping algebra of Lie algebra $\gL$. Define $\btl \colon \UgL \otimes \OO(G) \to \UgL$ by  $D \btl f = \epsilon(D_{(1)} (f)) D_{(2)}$. Prove that this is a right Hopf action. This induces a smash product algebra structure $\OO(G) \sharp \UgL$ on tensor product $\OO(G) \otimes \UgL$. Define $\genfd$-linear antimultiplicative map $\lambda \colon \UgL \to \OO(G) \sharp \UgL$ by $X_i \mapsto \bar\OO^i_j\sharp X_i$, prove that this is well defined and a left coaction. Prove braided commutativity. Now, from the property that $\bar\OO^i_j X_i$ is a right invariant derivation, the Yetter--Drinfeld property follows. We have that $\UgL$ is a braided commutative Yetter--Drinfeld module algebra over $\OO(G)$  and that $\OO(G)\sharp \UgL$ is a Hopf algebroid over $\UgL^{\op},\UgL$.
%%%%
%%%%Likewise, one can denote by $\gR$ the $\genfd$-vector subspace of $\mathcal{D}(G)$ generated by derivations $Y_1,\ldots, Y_n$. Similarly, one can define $\btl' \colon \UgR \otimes \OO(G) \to \UgR$ by  $D \btl' f = \epsilon(D_{(1)} (f)) D_{(2)}$. Prove that this is a right Hopf action of Hopf algebra $\OO(G)^\co$. This induces a smash product algebra structure $\OO(G)^\co \sharp \UgR$ on tensor product $\OO(G) \otimes \UgL$. Define $\genfd$-linear antimultiplicative map $\lambda' \colon \UgR \to \OO(G) \sharp \UgR$ by $Y_i \mapsto \OO^i_j\sharp Y_i$, prove that this is well defined and a left coaction. Prove braided commutativity. Now, from the property that $\OO^i_j Y_i$ is a left invariant derivation, the Yetter--Drinfeld property follows. We have that $\UgR$ is a braided commutative Yetter--Drinfeld module algebra over $\OO(G)^\co$ and that $\OO(G)^\co\sharp \UgR$ is a Hopf algebroid over $\UgR^{\op},\UgR$.
