\section{Introduction}
\label{Sec_Intro}

\pagestyle{plain}
\thispagestyle{plain}

Revenue maximization for a multiproduct monopoly is notoriously difficult. Even seemingly simple cases involving just two items remain poorly understood, despite decades of research (\citet[p. 735]{daskalakis2017strong}). Known proofs of optimality of even a two-dimensional uniform distribution are involved, while the optimal mechanisms for other simple distributions may require probabilistic bundling with a menu of infinitely many bundles.\footnote{See, for instance, \citet{pavlov2011optimal,giannakopoulos2018duality}, and \citet{thirumulanathan2019optimal2} for proofs regarding uniformly distributed products. See also \citet{daskalakis2017strong} and \citet{giannakopoulos2018selling} for distributions under which optimal mechanisms have infinite menu size.} In contrast, revenue maximization for a single-product monopolist is not only well understood---the optimal mechanism is to post a take-it-or-leave-it price---but can also be derived through applying standard marginal analysis to third-degree price discrimination, free of any mechanism design machinery; see \citet{bulow1989simple}.

In this paper, we revisit the multiproduct monopoly problem with the insight of \citet{bulow1989simple}. We adopt the taxation principle to recast the revenue-maximization problem as solving nonlinear pricing for bundles. The taxation principle, like the revelation principle, bears no loss in the class of implementable mechanisms, but unlike the revelation principle, it flips the (IC-)constrained optimization problem into pricing bundles self-selected by buyer types drawn from a given distribution. A taxation mechanism attaches prices to bundles of any fractions of each good and thereby generalizes what \citet{manelli2006bundling} called a price schedule which only prices bundles of goods in whole units. A fractional unit can be flexibly interpreted as quantity, quality, or probability.

We solve the bundle pricing problem by performing standard demand analysis. Different from \citet{bulow1989simple}, we focus on determining the marginal revenue (MR) with respect to a price perturbation rather than a quantity perturbation.\footnote{Only a few papers approach mechanism design problems with MR analysis. \citet[Theorem 1]{manelli2006bundling} can be viewed as a marginal revenue condition for \emph{deterministic} mechanisms. \citet{jehiel2007mixed} study an auction problem and analyze the marginal effects on welfare and revenue with respect to each \emph{deterministic} allocation, based on extending an early result of \citet{roberts1979characterization}.}
Given any optimal taxation mechanism, holding the prices of other bundles fixed, total revenue must not increase with any small price change for bundles with positive demand ($\MR=0$), nor with a small price decrease for bundles with zero demand ($\MR \geq 0$). When there is only one item, each quantity is demanded by an interval of buyer types and types in a higher interval demand a higher quantity. For any interior quantity, the resulting MR has a density equal to the derivative of the buyer's virtual value times the density of a type. When the MR density is positive, an optimal mechanism sells only the ``corner quantity'', i.e., the whole unit. Hence, the MR analysis recovers the optimality of a posted-price mechanism. In selling multiple items, when we perturb the price of a bundle, the set of types purchasing a bundle no longer forms an interval but can still be approximated by hypercubes and thereby the MR density coincides with the density of the transformed measure in \citet{daskalakis2017strong}. 

The MR analysis differs from the optimal transport approach proposed by \citet{daskalakis2017strong} in several aspects. First, the condition $\MR = 0$ for bundles with positive demand (hereafter, FOC) is directly formulated in terms of the transformed measure of \citet{daskalakis2017strong}. In contrast, their optimal transport problem requires solving a multidimensional signed measure whose positive (resp. negative) part convexly dominates the positive (resp. negative) part of the transformed measure. Second, the condition $\MR \geq 0$ for bundles with zero demand (hereafter, SOC) immediately follows from the marginal analysis but admits no direct counterpart in the optimal transport approach of \citet{daskalakis2017strong}. Third, both FOC and SOC apply to any convex type space with convex allocation spaces, whereas the strong duality framework in \citet{daskalakis2017strong} requires further generalization to accommodate such settings; see \citet{kash2016optimal}.\footnote{\citet{kleiner2019strong} provides a shorter alternative proof of the strong duality in \citet{daskalakis2017strong} with additive valuations drawn from $[0,1]^N$.} Finally, while FOC and SOC are not sufficient conditions for optimality, their necessity is standard; see Section \ref{Sec_General}. Moreover, we demonstrate that FOC and SOC fully identify optimal mechanisms in some cases and partially identify their features in other cases. 

The combined force of FOC and SOC substantially enhances our understanding of the optimal selling mechanisms with two items. To wit, we investigate symmetric two-dimensional joint distributions that induce a positive MR density—referred to hereafter as SRS distributions—which may be independent or correlated.\footnote{Strict regularity amounts to requiring a strict version of the hazard rate condition imposed by \citet{mcafee1988multidimensional} to extend the ``no-haggling result'' of \citet{maskin1984monopoly} to selling two items. It was discovered later by \citet{thanassoulis2004haggling} and \citet{manelli2006bundling} among others that revenue maximization for some strictly regular distributions still requires randomization.} For SRS distributions, FOC and SOC produce similar implications as with one item, namely that the optimal mechanism must sell a whole unit for \emph{at least one good}, which was documented by \citet{mcafee1988multidimensional} using a different argument. Moreover, across different $(q,1)$'s, each type $(x_1,x_2)$ above the 45-degree line makes the same purchase decision as its projection $(x_1,1)$; hence, the indirect utility of type $(x_1,x_2)$ can also be expressed in terms of the indirect utility function $\overline{u}$ of $(x_1,1)$ as $\overline{u}(x_1)-(1-x_2)$. As a result, the bundle $(\overline{u}'(x_1),1)$ is not only consumed by a boundary type $(x_1,1)$ (by the envelope condition) but also interior types $(x_1,x_2)$ up to where their participation constraint is binding, namely, $x_2=1-\overline{u}(x_1)$. Then, solving the optimal mechanism amounts to solving the one-dimensional indirect utility function $\overline{u}$.

While the literature proceeds from here with different approaches customized for different distributional assumptions, FOC and SOC offer a unified treatment. Specifically, each SRS type distribution identifies a threshold function $\zeta(x_1)$ at which the MR at $x_1$ is equal to zero. For instance, $\zeta(x_1)$ is equal to $2/3$ for the two-dimensional uniform distribution. According to $\zeta$, we have an MR deficit (surplus) if $1-\overline{u}$ is above (below) $\zeta$. As the types in the top-right corner and above the 45-degree line run an MR deficit, it follows from SOC that the grand bundle must have a positive demand. $\MR=0$ for the grand bundle pins down the cutoff $(2-\sqrt{2})/3$ at which the MR surplus exactly offsets the MR deficit; see panel (c) of Figure \ref{Fig_Uniform}. Moreover, it is impossible to have bundle(s) $(q,1)$ with $q>0$ and yet a positive demand; otherwise, its MR is zero (FOC), which requires an MR deficit on the left to cancel the MR surplus on the right. Then, since the indirect utility function $\overline{u}$ is nondecreasing and convex, selling any quantity smaller than $q$ of good 1 can have neither a zero demand (by SOC) nor a positive demand (by FOC); see panels (a) and (b) of Figure \ref{Fig_Uniform}. Hence, the optimal mechanism requires no randomization and the optimal $\overline{u}$ is uniquely pinned down by the cutoff $(2-\sqrt{2})/3$.

