The cross-subsidization of MR is reminiscent of “bunching” or “ironing” in one-dimensional screening problems. However, in the two-dimensional setting, the function $\overline{u}$ simultaneously determines both the menu of bundles and the endogenous, type-dependent participation constraints. The one-dimensional trade-off between $1-\overline{u}$ and $\zeta$ sidesteps the verification of convex dominance between multidimensional measures in \citet{daskalakis2017strong} and guides the optimal bundle selection from the threshold function $\zeta$ that can be computed from the type distribution.\footnote{We may think of $\zeta$ as a ''preliminary transportation plan'' shipping the negative MR in the interior to the positive MR at the top boundary or right boundary. Note however that ``ironing'' here pertains to the primal variables $\overline{u}$ of the revenue maximization problem, whereas the convex dominance in \citet{daskalakis2017strong} pertains to their dual variables.} First, when $\zeta(.)$ is a nondecreasing function, as assumed in \citet{manelli2006bundling}, \citet{hart2010revenue}, and \citet{tang2017optimal}, the MR conditions imply that the support of $\overline{u}'(.)$ is degenerate, namely, the optimal mechanism is deterministic. Next, when $\zeta$ is nonincreasing and concave, as in Example 3 of \citet{daskalakis2017strong} and assumed in \citet{giannakopoulos2018selling}, the optimal one-dimensional indirect utility function $\overline{u}$ is characterized by a cutoff $a^1$ such that $1-\overline{u}$ coincides with $\zeta$ for all $x_1 < a^1$, and $\overline{u}'(x_1)=1$ for all $x_1 \geq a^1$. When $\zeta$ is strictly concave, the optimal $\overline{u}$ induces bundles with infinitely many different fractional units of good 1 and thereby ``probabilistic bundling''. Both classes of distributions include the uniform distribution and confirm that its optimal mechanism is deterministic and $1-\overline{u}$ coincides with $\zeta$ to the left of $(2-\sqrt{2})/3$.

Beyond the two classes of distributions, the same analysis also fully identifies optimal mechanisms for other prominent distributions that have not been solved previously. First, when $\zeta$ is concave but not monotone, which is the case for a truncated normal distribution, we show that $\overline{u}$ is characterized by at most two cutoffs $b^0$ and $a^1$ such that $1-\overline{u}$ has slope $0$ for $x_1 < b^0$ and $-1$ for $x_1 \geq a_1$; moreover, $1-\overline{u}$ coincides with $\zeta$ in between. In particular, for truncated normal distributions, $\zeta$ is strictly concave with a peak equal to one half of its mean. Hence, when the mean is zero, the optimal mechanism necessitates probabilistic bundling with an infinite menu size. In contrast, when the mean is positive, the two cutoff values coincide, and the optimal mechanism simplifies to a deterministic one (mixed bundling).\footnote{\citet{schmalensee1984gaussian} pioneered the investigation of bundling strategies for bivariate normal distributions (without truncation) but he restricted attention to pure bundling and mixed bundling.} Second, when $\zeta$ is convex, which is the case for a truncated Pareto distribution, the mechanism sells at most two bundles: a grand bundle for types to the left of a cutoff $a^1$ and a bundle $(q,1)$ with $q<1$ for types to the right of $a^1$. Again, when $q>0$, the optimal mechanism requires randomization, but the menu size is finite. In general, as long as $\zeta''(.)$ does not switch sign too frequently, the MR conditions reduce solving the optimal indirect utility function to solving an optimization problem with few variables. The feature closely resembles solving partitional signals for information design.\footnote{See, for instance, \citet{roesler2017buyer} , \citet{dworczak2019simple}, \citet{kleiner2021extreme}, \citet{chen2023information}, and \citet{bergemann2024bidder}.}

When we restrict the allocation space to reflect complementarity or substitution between the two goods, the same analysis applies and generates different insights. Specifically, we parametrize the buyer's utility from consuming the grand bundle as $\mathbf{x}\cdot(k,k)$ and consider pricing allocations in the convex hull $\mathcal{Q}$ of $\{(0,0)$, $(1,0)$, $(0,1)$, and $(k,k)\}$. When $k=1/2$, the two goods are perfect substitutes and the buyer has a unit demand. When $1/2<k<1$ ($k>1$), the two goods are partial substitutes (complements), as consuming both goods yields a higher (lower) utility than the total utility of consuming them separately. Since the designer still invokes boundary allocations to screen the buyer's types, the threshold function $\zeta_k$ should be computed via integrating the MR density in a direction orthogonal to the allocation boundary. For $k=1/2$, we now have an MR deficit at the top-left corner as shown in panel (a) of Figure \ref{Fig_Uniform_k}; hence, SOC implies that $(0,1)$ must be active, in parallel to the grand bundle for $k=1$. Moreover, FOC and SOC imply that the optimal mechanism must only sell $(0,1)$ and $(1,0)$, as documented in \citet{pavlov2011optimal}. When $k>1$, the grand bundle is again active, but since the allocation boundary is tilted counterclockwise, FOC is satisfied before reaching the cutoff $x_1=(2-\sqrt{2})/3$ for $k=1$. As shown in panel (c) of Figure \ref{Fig_Uniform_k}, the bundle $(0,1)$ cannot satisfy FOC and thereby must be inactive. Moreover, if $k<1+\sqrt{2/3}$, then pure bundling is not optimal and optimal mechanisms involve probabilistic bundling, which complements the finding of \citet{kash2016optimal} for $k=2$. In Section \ref{Sec_SubsComp}, we apply the same approach to characterize the optimal mechanism for any $k$ and different shapes of $\zeta_k$. Tables \ref{Tab_Intro_1} and \ref{Tab_Intro_2} contrast our findings with prior work, showing how MR analysis breaks new ground. 

