For general distributions and/or more than two items, FOC and SOC also reveal novel features of the optimal selling mechanisms. To demonstrate this point, we provide two results for arbitrary convex type spaces and convex allocation spaces. First, we show that as long as the type distribution remains strictly regular, only a subset of allocations on the boundary are possibly active. These are allocations such that any increase in a direction perpendicular to the outward boundary of the type space renders them infeasible. This result generalizes a similar result of \citet{pavlov2011optimal} for a rectangular type space and allows us to obtain a corresponding result of \citet{bikhchandani2022selling} for their triangular type space. Second, without requiring strict regularity, we show that a boundary allocation must be active if some normal vector associated with an exposed face of the type space lies in the interior of the outward normal cone at $\mathbf{q}$ to the allocation space. They include both a grand bundle for the case with additive valuations and separate selling bundles for the case with a unit demand.

The sequel of the paper is organized as follows. \Cref{Sec_1D} offers a step-by-step derivation of marginal revenue for a single-product monopoly. \Cref{Sec_2D} presents our main results for a two-product monopoly. \Cref{Sec_SubsComp} extends the analysis in \Cref{Sec_2D} by allowing the two products to be either substitutes or complements. \Cref{Sec_General} provides general results for an arbitrary number of products. \Cref{Sec_Conclusion} concludes. Some proofs and discussions are relegated to the Appendix.

\section{Single-Product Monopoly}
\label{Sec_1D}

Consider a market that comprises a seller (she) and a unit mass of buyers (he). The seller produces a single good with production costs normalized to zero. Each buyer needs at most one unit of the good and receives utility $x\in \mathcal{X}=[0,1]$ from consuming the good. The seller cannot observe $x$ but knows that $x$ is drawn from a distribution with probability density function (p.d.f.) $f$. We assume that $f$ is continuously differentiable on the interior of $\mathcal{X}$ and that $f(1)>0$.

As a starting point, assume that the good is divided into $K$ equal pieces, where $K\in \mathbb{N}_{+}$. Thus, the set of feasible quantities $\{i/K:i\in\{0,1,\dotsc,K\}\}$ is finite. We will sometimes omit $1/K$ and simply use $i$ to represent a quantity. The seller chooses a \emph{price schedule} (or mechanism) $\mathbf{p}=\{p(i)\}_{0\leq i\leq K}$, where $p(i)$ is the price for quantity $i$. Assume that buyers have zero reservation utility, so it is without loss of generality to set $p(0)=0$.

Given a price schedule $\mathbf{p}$, the \emph{demand set} of $i$ is the set of buyers that weakly prefer $i$:
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{D}(i;\mathbf{p})=\left\{x\in \mathcal{X}: \frac{xi}{K}-p(i)\geq \frac{x\hat{i}}{K}-p(\hat{i})\text{ for all }\hat{i}\right\}.
\end{equation*}
The \emph{demand} for $i$ is the mass of buyers that weakly prefer $i$:
\begin{equation*}
D(i;\mathbf{p})=\int_{\mathcal{D}(i;\mathbf{p})}f(x)\dd x.
\end{equation*}
For any $i>0$, we say that $i$ is \emph{active} if $D(i;\mathbf{p})>0$ and \emph{inactive} if $D(i;\mathbf{p})=0$. Let $\mathbf{p}^{-i}$ be the vector of prices excluding $p(i)$, and
\begin{equation*}
\overline{p}(i;\mathbf{p}^{-i})=\sup\{p(i):D(i;p(i),\mathbf{p}^{-i})>0\}.
\end{equation*}
Then $i$ is active if and only if $p(i)<\overline{p}(i;\mathbf{p}^{-i})$. To rule out unbounded prices, we assume that the seller can only choose price schedules satisfying $p(i)\leq\overline{p}(i;\mathbf{p}^{-i})$. In other words, $i$ is inactive if and only if $p(i)=\overline{p}(i;\mathbf{p}^{-i})$.\footnote{When $0<i<K$, $\overline{p}(i;\mathbf{p}^{-i})$ is the convex lower envelope of $\mathbf{p}^{-i}$ evaluated at $i$. Therefore, $p(i)\leq \overline{p}(i;\mathbf{p}^{-i})$ essentially requires $p$ to be convex, even though convexity is not explicitly imposed as a constraint.} The \emph{demand profile} is the vector of demand functions $\mathbf{D}(\mathbf{p})=\{D(i;\mathbf{p})\}_{1\leq i\leq K}$. The seller's total revenue is simply
\begin{equation*}
\TR (\mathbf{p})=\mathbf{p}\cdot \mathbf{D}(\mathbf{p}).
\end{equation*}
A price schedule is \emph{optimal} if it maximizes $\TR (\mathbf{p})$.

If $i$ is active, then $\mathcal{D}(i;\mathbf{p})$ is a closed interval, which we denote by $\mathcal{D}(i;\mathbf{p})=[a^{i},b^{i}]$. Assume that $0<a^{i}<b^{i}<1$, which implies $0<i<K$. A type-$a^{i}$ buyer is indifferent between $i$ and $i-1$, and a type-$b^{i}$ buyer is indifferent between $i$ and $i+1$.\footnote{That buyers are indifferent between adjacent bundles is due to $p(i)\leq \overline{p}(i;\mathbf{p}^{-i})$.}

Consider increasing $p(i)$ by a small amount $\varepsilon>0$ while ensuring that $i$ remains active. The direct effect of this price increase is an additional revenue $\varepsilon D(i;\mathbf{p})$. The indirect effect comes from altering buyers' purchasing decisions at the endpoints of $\mathcal{D}(i;\mathbf{p})$. Buyers with $a^{i}<x<a^{i}+\varepsilon K$ will switch to purchasing $i-1$, leading to a change of revenue
\begin{equation*}
(p(i-1)-p(i))\int^{a^{i}+\varepsilon K}_{a^{i}}f(x)\dd x\approx -\varepsilon K(p(i)-p(i-1))f(a^{i})=-\varepsilon a^{i}f(a^{i}),
\end{equation*}
where the last equality comes from $a^{i}i/K-p(i)=a^{i}(i-1)/K-p(i-1)$.
Similarly, buyers with $b^{i}-\varepsilon K<x<b^{i}$ will switch to purchasing $i+1$, leading to a change of revenue
\begin{equation*}
(p(i+1)-p(i))\int^{b^{i}}_{b^{i}-\varepsilon K}f(x)\dd x\approx \varepsilon K(p(i+1)-p(i))f(b^{i})=\varepsilon b^{i}f(b^{i}).
\end{equation*}
The total effect of this price change is
\begin{equation*}
\varepsilon \int^{b^{i}}_{a^{i}}f(x)\dd x-\varepsilon a^{i}f(a^{i})+\varepsilon b^{i}f(b^{i})=\varepsilon \int^{b^{i}}_{a^{i}}(xf'(x)+2f(x))\dd x.
\end{equation*}
Let $\phi(x)=xf'(x)+2f(x)$. Then the \emph{marginal revenue} with respect to $p(i)$ is
\begin{equation}
\label{1D_MR}
\MR (i;\mathbf{p})=\int^{b^{i}}_{a^{i}}\phi(x)\dd x,
\end{equation}

Recall that the analysis relies on our assumption $0<a^{i}<b^{i}<1$. If $a^{i}=0$, type-0 buyers will not alter their purchasing decisions after the price change. That is, we should omit $-a^{i}f(a^{i})$ when computing MR, but this does not change the formulation in \eqref{1D_MR}. If $b^{i}=1$, type-1 buyers will not alter their purchasing decisions after the price change, so we have to revise \eqref{1D_MR} to
\begin{equation*}
\MR (i;\mathbf{p})=\int^{1}_{a^{i}}\phi(x)\dd x-f(1).
\end{equation*}
Clearly, a price schedule is optimal only if the marginal revenue equals zero for any active quantity.

We say that $f$ is \emph{strictly regular} if $\phi(x)>0$ for almost all $x\in [0,1]$. In this case, $\int^{b}_{a}\phi(x)\dd x>0$ for any $0\leq a<b\leq 1$. Hence, $K$ is the only active quantity and the mechanism is a posted price (for the whole item). This result is stated in \Cref{Prop_1D}.

\setcounter{proposition}{-1}
\begin{proposition}
\label{Prop_1D}
Suppose that $f$ is strictly regular. Then a posted price mechanism is optimal.
\end{proposition}

Although \Cref{Prop_1D} is well-known, here it is derived using a marginal revenue approach that differs from the classical literature, such as \citet{bulow1989simple} and \citet{wilson1993nonlinear}. In our paper, MR is computed by perturbing the price schedule, while in \citet{bulow1989simple}, MR is defined as the derivative of total revenue with respect to quantity. As will become clear in Sections \ref{Sec_2D}--\ref{Sec_General}, when the seller has multiple products, her total revenue is no longer the integral of the allocation rule times the virtual value.\footnote{\citet{rochet2003economics} use an auxiliary function to express total revenue as an integral of the allocation rule. However, this auxiliary function is endogenous to the optimal mechanism and is usually difficult to characterize.} Hence, the quantity-based MR cannot be easily extended to multiproduct monopoly, as the appropriate definition of virtual value remains unclear.

Another important distinction between our paper and \citet{bulow1989simple} is that we consider a finite partition of the allocation space and define MR for each allocation, while \citet{bulow1989simple} only consider MR with respect to the total quantity and assume away randomization. In this regard, our approach is closer to \citet{wilson1993nonlinear}, which requires the seller to specify a price for every \emph{incremental} quantity. As a result, a buyer who purchases the $i$-th increment must also purchase all preceding increments, and the price schedule must be nonincreasing in quantity. In contrast, our approach is based on the self-selection of buyers and works without the monotonicity constraint on the price schedule.

