This section extends the marginal revenue approach in \Cref{Sec_1D} to a two-product monopoly. \Cref{Sec_2D_Finite} focuses on a finite quantity space, while \Cref{Sec_2D_Continuum} extends the same setting to a continuum of quantities.

\subsection{A Finite Number of Quantities}
\label{Sec_2D_Finite}

To avoid repetition, we only summarize settings that are different from \Cref{Sec_1D}. Now the seller produces two goods, and each buyer needs at most one unit of each good. A buyer's utility from consuming each good is additive and is represented by a vector $\mathbf{x}\in \mathcal{X}=[0,1]^{2}$.\footnote{As a convention, we use bold fonts, such as $\mathbf{x}$, to represent vectors, and use $x_{n}$ to represent the $n$-th dimension of $\mathbf{x}$. We also let $\mathbf{c}=(c,c)$, $\mathbf{e}_{1}=(1,0)$, and $\mathbf{e}_{2}=(0,1)$.} Again, we assume that the p.d.f. of the type distribution $f$ is continuously differentiable on the interior of $\mathcal{X}$ and that $f(\mathbf{x})>0$ for almost all $\mathbf{x}$ with $x_{1}=1$ or $x_{2}=1$.

We use $(i,j)$ to represent a bundle with $i/K$ units of good 1 and $j/K$ units of good 2, where $i,j\in \{0,\dotsc,K\}$. The seller chooses a price schedule $\mathbf{p}=\{p(i,j)\}_{i,j\in \{0,1,\dotsc, K\}}$, where $p(i,j)$ is the price for bundle $(i,j)$ and $p(\mathbf{0})=0$. To simplify notation, we will omit $\mathbf{p}$ from the arguments of $\mathcal{D}$ and $\MR$ when there is no risk of ambiguity. The demand set of $(i,j)$ is
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{D}(i,j)=\left\{\mathbf{x}\in \mathcal{X}:\frac{x_{1}i+x_{2}j}{K}-p(i,j)\geq \frac{x_{1}\hat{i}+x_{2}\hat{j}}{K}-p(\hat{i},\hat{j})\text{ for all }\hat{i},\hat{j}\right\}.
\end{equation*}
The definitions of active (and inactive) bundle, demand profile, total revenue, and optimal price schedule are analogous to those \Cref{Sec_1D} and are thus omitted.

Just as the single-product monopoly, a price schedule is optimal only if the marginal revenue equals zero for any active bundle. Without further reasoning, we present the expression of marginal revenue for any $(i,j)$:
\begin{equation}
\label{Eqn_MR_2D}
\MR (i,j)=\int_{\mathcal{D}(i,j)}\phi(\mathbf{x})\dd \mathbf{x}-\int_{\mathcal{D}(i,j)\cap \overline{\bd}(\mathcal{X})}f(\mathbf{x})(\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{n}_{\mathbf{x}})\dd \sigma (\mathbf{x}),
\end{equation}
where $\phi(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{x}\cdot\nabla f(\mathbf{x})+3f(\mathbf{x})$, $\mathbf{n}_{\mathbf{x}}$ is the outward unit normal to $\mathcal{X}$ at $\mathbf{x}$, $\overline{\bd}(\mathcal{X})=\{\mathbf{x}\in \mathcal{X}: x_{1}=1\text{ or }x_{2}=1\}$ is the \emph{top boundary} of $\mathcal{X}$, and $\sigma (\mathbf{x})$ is the line integral on $\overline{\bd}(\mathcal{X})$. A detailed derivation of MR can be found in \Cref{App_MR}.

We need two assumptions to characterize the set of active bundles. First, assume that $f$ is strictly regular, that is, $\phi(\mathbf{x})>0$ for almost all $\mathbf{x}$.\footnote{The \emph{weak} regularity assumption, meaning that $\phi (\mathbf{x})\geq 0$ for almost all $\mathbf{x}\in \mathcal{X}$, is commonly used in the literature of multiproduct monopoly. See, for instance, \citet{mcafee1988multidimensional,manelli2006bundling,hart2010revenue,pavlov2011optimal,pavlov2011property,menicucci2015optimality,tang2017optimal,giannakopoulos2018selling}, and \citet{bikhchandani2022selling}. We assume strict regularity to ensure that our results hold for all optimal mechanisms and that the $\zeta$ function introduced later is well-defined.\label{Ftn_Reg}} In this case, we have a simple extension of \Cref{Prop_1D}:

\begin{observation}
\label{Obs_1}
If $(i,j)$ is active, then $i=K$ or $j=K$.
\end{observation}

Second, assume that $f$ is symmetric, that is, $f(x_{1},x_{2})=f(x_{2},x_{1})$. As a result, it is without loss of generality to focus on symmetric mechanisms:

\begin{observation}
\label{Obs_2}
If $(i,K)$ is active, then $(K,i)$ is also active.
\end{observation}

We say that the environment is \emph{strictly regular and symmetric (SRS)} if $f$ is strictly regular and symmetric. These two assumptions are maintained till the end of this section.

Observations \ref{Obs_1} and \ref{Obs_2} are not enough to pin down an optimal mechanism. To obtain further results, we need to study buyers' indirect utility function induced by $p$:
\begin{equation*}
u(\mathbf{x})=\sup_{0\leq i,j\leq K}\left\{\frac{x_{1}i+x_{2}j}{K}-p(i,j)\right\}.
\end{equation*}
We will also refer to $u$ as a mechanism when there is no risk of ambiguity. Since $f$ is symmetric, it is without loss of generality to focus on symmetric $u$. Consider a type-$\mathbf{x}$ buyer with $x_{1}\leq x_{2}$ and $u(\mathbf{x})>0$. By Observations \ref{Obs_1} and \ref{Obs_2}, he will only choose between $\{(i,K)\}_{1\leq i\leq K}$, and his choice will be the same as a type-$(x_{1},1)$ buyer. If we let $\overline{u}(\cdot)=u(\cdot,1)=u(1,\cdot)$, then the type-$\mathbf{x}$ buyer's indirect utility can be formulated as $u(\mathbf{x})=\overline{u}(x_{1})-(1-x_{2})$. Relaxing the assumptions $x_{1}\leq x_{2}$ and $u(\mathbf{x})>0$, we have
\begin{equation}
\label{Eqn_2D_MM}
u(\mathbf{x})=\max\{\overline{u}(x_{1})-(1-x_{2}),\overline{u}(x_{2})-(1-x_{1}),0\}.
\end{equation}
Let $[a^{i},b^{i}]=\{x\in [0,1]:\overline{u}'(x-)\leq i/K\leq \overline{u}'(x+)\}$. When $i<k$, the demand set of $(i,K)$ is
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{D}(i,K)=\{\mathbf{x}\in \mathcal{X}: a^{i}\leq x_{1}\leq b^{i}\text{, }\max (1-\overline{u}(x_{1}),x_{1})\leq x_{2}\leq 1\}.
\end{equation*}

\Cref{Fig_Discrete} illustrates one possible configuration of how the demand sets are positioned within the type space. The red curve is $1-\overline{u}(x_{1})$ and its symmetric counterpart $1-\overline{u}(x_{2})$. Demand sets are separated by dashed lines.

