As a special case of \Cref{Cor_Ccv}, when $\zeta$ is nondecreasing and concave, $\max(1-\overline{u}(b^{0}),b^{0})\leq \zeta (b^{0})$ implies $\max (1-\overline{u}(0),0)\leq \zeta (0)$. Thus, $\mathbf{e}_{2}$ is pooling only if $1-\overline{u}(x)=\zeta (x)$ for all $x\in [0,b^{0}]$. Hence, we actually have $\overline{u}(x)=1-\zeta (x)$ for all $x\in [0,a^{1}]$. The optimal $\overline{u}$ ``follows'' $1-\zeta$ until it reaches the smallest point where $\overline{u}'(x+)=1$. This case illustrates the intuition that $\zeta$ is an unconstrained pointwise solution to the condition $\MR=0$. If $1-\zeta$ happens to share the same properties as a feasible $\overline{u}$, then it becomes a natural candidate for the optimal $\overline{u}$. Due to its importance, we say that $f$ (or $\zeta$) is \emph{double-regular} if $\zeta$ is nonincreasing and concave on $[0,1]$ and make a formal statement in \Cref{Cor_CcvDec}.

\begin{corollary}
\label{Cor_CcvDec}
Suppose that $\zeta$ is double-regular. Then in any symmetric optimal mechanism, there exists $a^{1}\in [0,1)$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\overline{u}(x)=1-\zeta (x)$ for $x\in [0,a^{1})$,
\item $\overline{u}'(x)=1$ for $x\in [a^{1},1]$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{corollary}

\Cref{Fig_Ccv} graphically illustrates Corollaries \ref{Cor_Ccv} and \ref{Cor_CcvDec}. In panel (a), since $\zeta$ may be strictly increasing when $x$ is small, it is possible that $1-\overline{u}$ cannot follow $\zeta$ when $x<b^{0}$. In this case, we may need to ``iron'' MR, so pooling arises naturally in optimal mechanisms. In panel (b), $b^{0}=a^{1}$, $1-\overline{u}$ does not follow $\zeta$ on the entire $[0,1]$, meaning that all active bundles are pooling. In panel (c), $\zeta$ is double-regular, so $1-\overline{u}=\zeta$ on $[0,a^{1}]$.

