\begin{example}[Truncated normal distribution]
\label{Exp_NormalPositive}
Let $g(x)=Ce^{-(x-\theta)^{2}/2}$, where $\theta\in [0,2]$, and $C$ is a normalization constant. Then $h(x)=x(\theta-x)\geq -1$ for all $x\in [0,1]$, so $g$ is strictly regular. It can be proved that $\zeta$ is strictly concave with a peak at $\theta/2$. When $\theta=0$, $\zeta$ is double-regular.\footnote{We numerically solve for the cutoffs $b^{0}$ and $a^{1}$ and find that optimal mechanisms exhibit infinite menu size (panel (c) of \Cref{Fig_Ccv}) when $\theta=0$ and are deterministic (panel (b) of \Cref{Fig_Ccv}) for $\theta\in (0,2]$. In other words, there is a sudden change from infinite menu size to mixed bundling at $\theta=0$. Moreover, when we set $\theta=0$ but allow for any positive correlation between $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$, optimal mechanisms are also deterministic.} 
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Truncated Gamma distribution]
\label{Exp_Gamma}
Let $g(x)=Cx^{\eta-1}e^{-\lambda x}$, where $\lambda \in (0,1]$, $\eta \geq \lambda$, and $C$ is a normalization constant. Then $h(x)=\eta-1-\lambda x\geq -1$ for all $x\in [0,1]$, so $g$ is strictly regular. It can be proved that $\zeta$ is strictly decreasing and strictly concave.
\end{example}

Without assuming regularity, \citet{daskalakis2017strong} posit the existence of two nonincreasing and concave functions that serve a similar role to $\zeta$ in our framework. These functions allow one to construct a solution to their dual optimal transport problem, but it remains unclear how to identify such functions in general. \citet{giannakopoulos2018selling} study nonincreasing and concave $\zeta$ under a similar regularity assumption and use it to solve an alternative dual problem. However, they only establish weak duality and do not extend their method to other shapes of $\zeta$. They also miss the connection between $\zeta$ and $\MR$ and thus cannot conclude that the grand bundle $\mathbf{1}$ is always active.

\subsection{Summary}

It is worth noting that Corollaries \ref{Cor_Inc} and \ref{Cor_CcvDec} are \emph{complete} characterizations of optimal mechanisms, as the only cutoff $a^{1}$ in both corollaries can be pinned down by $\MR (\mathbf{1})=0$. Corollaries \ref{Cor_Cvx} and \ref{Cor_Ccv} introduce an additional degree of freedom---$q$ or $b^{0}$, respectively---in addition to $a^{1}$, meaning that one still needs to explicitly solve for the two cutoffs. However, this final step is arguably much more tractable than solving for optimal mechanisms directly. Finally, when $\zeta$ does not exhibit a desirable global property as those listed in Corollaries \ref{Cor_Inc}--\ref{Cor_CcvDec}, we can still find an upper bound on the number of pooling bundles in any optimal mechanism. This result is presented in \Cref{Sec_MenuSize}.

Although some of the corollaries and examples in this section have been studied in the literature, we are not aware of any existing work that derives all these results within a unified framework. In fact, finding optimal mechanisms when types follow normal or Gamma distributions has attracted significant attention from the literature.\footnote{See \citet{manelli2006bundling}. In early studies, \citet{schmalensee1984gaussian} and \citet{long1984comments} examine the seller's incentive to adopt pure or mixed bundling when buyers' type follows a normal distribution, but their analysis excludes randomization.} Examples \ref{Exp_NormalPositive} and \ref{Exp_Gamma} can be regarded as a first step toward a complete solution for these cases.

The marginal revenue approach provides an answer to an open question raised in \citet{daskalakis2017strong}: Are there broad conditions under which the optimal mechanism has a simple closed-form description or takes the form of \eqref{Eqn_2D_MM}? While it is known from \citet{mcafee1988multidimensional} that regularity is sufficient, there has been no systematic method for identifying optimal mechanisms based on primitives. Our analysis in \Cref{Sec_2D} demonstrates that the marginal revenue approach offers a compelling unified perspective. We will further explore its power in the following sections.

\section{Substitutes and Complements}
\label{Sec_SubsComp}

A crucial assumption in \Cref{Sec_2D} is that the buyer's utility from consuming the grand bundle equals the sum of his utilities from consuming the two products separately. In this section, we relax this assumption and extend the marginal revenue approach to a more general setting in which the two products may be substitutes or complements. To accommodate this change, we modify the model as follows.

Let $Y=\{\emptyset,\{1\},\{2\},\{1,2\}\}$ be the power set of items. Let $\mathbf{v}:Y\mapsto \mathbb{R}^{2}_{+}$ be a function that maps each subset of items to a vector of values.\footnote{Throughout the paper, we use $\mathbb{R}^{N}_{+}$ to represent the $N$-dimensional nonnegative orthant, that is, $\mathbb{R}^{N}_{+}=\{\mathbf{x}\in \mathbb{R}^{N}:x_{n}\geq 0\text{ for all }n\in \mathcal{N}\}$.} A type-$\mathbf{x}$ buyer's utility from consuming $y\in Y$ is $\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{v}(y)$. For any probability distribution $\pi$ over $Y$, we define $\mathbf{v}(\pi)=\E_{y\sim \pi}[\mathbf{v}(y)]$. The set of feasible allocations is the convex hull of $\{\mathbf{v}(y):y\in Y\}$, denoted by
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{Q}=\Conv \{\mathbf{v}(y):y\in Y\}=\{\mathbf{v}(\pi):\pi\in \Delta (Y)\}.
\end{equation*}
The price schedule is a function $p: \mathcal{Q}\mapsto \mathbb{R}$. For consistency, we will continue to refer to elements in $\mathcal{Q}$ as bundles, even though elements in $Y$ are literally closer to the meaning of this notion. The definitions of active/inactive and pooling/separating bundles remain the same as those in \Cref{Sec_2D}. However, it is important to note that they are all defined with respect to the elements of $\mathcal{Q}$.

To fix ideas, we assume $\mathbf{v}(\emptyset)=\mathbf{0}$, $\mathbf{v}(\{1\})=\mathbf{e}_{1}$, $\mathbf{v}(\{2\})=\mathbf{e}_{2}$, and $\mathbf{v}(\{1,2\})=\mathbf{k}$. Hence, the two products are perfect substitutes if $k\leq 1/2$, partial substitutes if $1/2<k<1$, and partial complements if $k>1$. Note that when $k<1/2$, the set of feasible allocations $\mathcal{Q}$ is the same as that of $k=1/2$, so we omit this case and focus solely on $k\geq 1/2$ in the following discussion. The type space is still $\mathcal{X}=[0,1]^{2}$, and the environment continues to be SRS.

In this new setting, part \ref{Prop_2D_1} of \Cref{Prop_2D} can be extended to characterize the set of (potentially) active bundles. Specifically, if $\mathbf{q}$ is active with $q_{1}\leq q_{2}$, then $q_{2}=(1-1/k)q_{1}+1$.\footnote{\citet{pavlov2011property} also characterizes the set of (potentially) active bundles when $\mathcal{X}$ is a hyperrectangle. See \Cref{Sec_Active} for a detailed discussion.} For different values of $k$, the three pictures at the top row of \Cref{Fig_SubsComp} illustrate the set of feasible allocations $\mathcal{Q}$ and highlight the (potentially) active bundles in red. Given these bundles, the three pictures at the bottom row of \Cref{Fig_SubsComp} use dashed red lines to depict the relationship between demand sets.

