\end{cases}
\end{equation*}

\begin{example}[Uniform distribution]
\label{Exp_Uniform_k}

Suppose that $f(\mathbf{x})=1$. Then the set of active bundles in optimal mechanisms varies with $k$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $k=1/2$, then only $\mathbf{e}_{1}$ and $\mathbf{e}_{2}$ are active.
\item If $1/2<k<1$, then only $\mathbf{e}_{1}$, $\mathbf{e}_{2}$, and $\mathbf{k}$ are active.
\item If $k>1$, then both $\mathbf{e}_{1}$ and $\mathbf{e}_{2}$ are inactive. If, in addition, $k<1+\sqrt{2/3}$, then pure bundling is not optimal. As a result, optimal mechanisms must have probabilistic bundling.
\end{enumerate}
\end{example}

A few papers study how to sell multiple products with non-additive values. For instance, \citet{armstrong2013more} derives sufficient conditions under which mixed bundling is preferred over separate selling but does not address the role of randomization. \citet{haghpanah2021pure,ghili2023characterization,yang2023nested}, and \citet{feng2025optimal} apply similar modeling techniques as our paper, but they either focus exclusively on one-dimensional types or impose assumptions that ensure types are ordered along a path. Consequently, their results differ from ours.\footnote{For example, suppose that a nested bundling mechanism as defined by \citet{yang2023nested} with only two active bundles, $\mathbf{e}_{1}$ and $\mathbf{1}$, is optimal. Then, a symmetric mechanism with three active bundles, $(1/2)\mathbf{e}_{1}$, $(1/2)\mathbf{e}_{2}$, and $\mathbf{1}$, must also be optimal. We can derive necessary conditions for this optimality by applying Propositions \ref{Prop_2D} and \ref{Prop_SubsComp}. See also \Cref{Sec_Verify_2D} for a detailed analysis.} When products are perfect substitutes ($k=1/2$), the model is commonly referred to as having \emph{unit-demand} buyers, and has been studied by \citet{thanassoulis2004haggling,pavlov2011optimal,pavlov2011property,thirumulanathan2019optimal1}, and \citet{hashimoto2025selling}.

To the best of our knowledge, our paper is the first to systematically solve the problem with multidimensional types and probabilistic allocations when products are partial substitutes ($1/2<k<1$) or complements ($k>1$).\footnote{\citet{kash2016optimal} extend the strong duality in \citet{daskalakis2017strong} to non-additive values and study an example where $k=2$ and types are uniformly distributed.} Specifically, \Cref{Exp_Uniform_k} reveals a new driving force for probabilistic allocation: When the complementarity between the two products is sufficiently small, that is, when $k$ is slightly greater than 1, deterministic mechanisms are suboptimal and randomization is therefore necessary.

\section{The General Model}
\label{Sec_General}

This section further extends model settings in Sections \ref{Sec_2D} and \ref{Sec_SubsComp} to accommodate a general type space. Now the seller has $N\in \mathbb{N}_{+}$ items, indexed by subscript $n\in \mathcal{N}=\{1,\dotsc,N\}$. Both the type space $\mathcal{X}$ and the allocation space $\mathcal{Q}$ are convex and compact subsets of $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{N}$, each with a nonempty interior and a piecewise smooth boundary.\footnote{More precisely, $\bd (\mathcal{X})$ and $\bd (\mathcal{Q})$ can be partitioned into finitely many smooth surfaces. While this assumption may be stronger than what is really necessary for the divergence theorem, we impose it primarily because it is satisfied by most of the applications in the literature.} Assume that $\mathbf{0}\in \mathcal{Q}$. We also impose the following assumptions on the p.d.f. of the type distribution $f$.

\begin{assumption}
\label{Asp_f}
\begin{enumerate}
\item $f$ is continuously differentiable on $\Int (\mathcal{X})$. \label{Asp_f_1}
\item $\lVert \mathbf{x} \rVert f(\mathbf{x})$ is bounded for almost all $\mathbf{x}\in \bd (\mathcal{X})$. \label{Asp_f_2}
\item $\phi (\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{x}\cdot \nabla f(\mathbf{x})+(N+1)f(\mathbf{x})$ is bounded for almost all $\mathbf{x}\in \Int (\mathcal{X})$. \label{Asp_f_3}
\item $f(\mathbf{x})>0$ for almost all $\mathbf{x}\in \bd (\mathcal{X})$. \label{Asp_f_4}
\end{enumerate}
\end{assumption}

In \ref{Asp_f_2} and \ref{Asp_f_4} of \Cref{Asp_f}, we extend the support of $\lVert \mathbf{x} \rVert f(\mathbf{x})$ and $f$ to $\bd (\mathcal{X})$ by continuity. \Cref{Thm_MR} only needs \ref{Asp_f_1}--\ref{Asp_f_3}, while Theorems \ref{Thm_Active} and \ref{Thm_Pooling} require \ref{Asp_f_1}--\ref{Asp_f_4}.

Again, the seller sets a price schedule (or a mechanism) $p:\mathcal{Q}\mapsto \mathbb{R}$ which assigns a take-it-or-leave-it price to each bundle $\mathbf{q}\in \mathcal{Q}$. The buyer can then freely choose any $\mathbf{q}$ and pay the corresponding price without reporting his type. A mechanism is \emph{feasible} if $p(\mathbf{0})=0$, which ensures that buyers receive nonnegative utility.\footnote{In fact, the price of the zero bundle can be nonpositive. Since we focus on the seller's revenue maximization problem, it is always optimal for the seller to increase $p(\mathbf{0})$ if it is negative. Thus, $p(\mathbf{0})=0$ is without loss of generality.} 

The indirect utility function induced by $p$ is the convex conjugate of $p$:
\begin{equation}
\label{IC_p}
u_{p}(\mathbf{x})=\sup_{\mathbf{q}\in \mathcal{Q}}\{\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}-p(\mathbf{q})\}.
\tag{IC$_{p}$}
\end{equation}
Similar to \Cref{Sec_2D}, a type-$\mathbf{x}$ buyer purchases $\mathbf{q}$ if $\mathbf{q}\in \partial u_{p}(\mathbf{x})$. The seller's total revenue is
\begin{equation*}
\TR (p)=\int_{\mathcal{X}}[\mathbf{x}\cdot \nabla u_{p}(\mathbf{x})-u_{p}(\mathbf{x})]f(\mathbf{x})\dd\mathbf{x}.
\end{equation*}
By the divergence theorem, the total revenue can be reformulated as a functional of $u_{p}$ (\citet{daskalakis2017strong}):
\begin{equation}
\label{Eqn_Rev}
\TR (p)=\int_{\mathcal{X}}[\mathbf{x}\cdot\nabla u_{p}(\mathbf{x})-u_{p}(\mathbf{x})]f(\mathbf{x})\dd \mathbf{x}=\int_{\mathcal{X}}u_{p}\dd \mu,
\end{equation}
where $\mu$ is a signed Radon measure defined as follows: For any measurable $\mathcal{A}\subseteq \mathcal{X}$,
\begin{equation}
\label{Eqn_mu}
\mu(\mathcal{A})=\int_{\mathcal{A}\cap \bd (\mathcal{X})}(\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{n}_{\mathbf{x}})f(\mathbf{x})\dd \sigma (\mathbf{x})-\int_{\mathcal{A}}\phi(\mathbf{x})\dd \mathbf{x},
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{n}_{\mathbf{x}}$ is the outward unit normal to $\bd (\mathcal{X})$ at $\mathbf{x}$, $\sigma (\mathbf{x})$ is the surface integral on $\bd (\mathcal{X})$.\footnote{Since $\bd (\mathcal{X})$ is piecewise smooth, the set of points where $\mathbf{n}_{\mathbf{x}}$ is not uniquely defined is $\sigma$-negligible.}
It can be verified that $\mu (\mathcal{X})=-\int_{\mathcal{X}}f(\mathbf{x})\dd\mathbf{x}=-1$. 

A full statement of the seller's revenue-maximization problem is:
\begin{equation}
\label{Eqn_Problem_p}
\begin{gathered}
\max_{p}\int_{\mathcal{X}}u_{p}\dd \mu,\\
\text{subject to \eqref{IC_p}.}
\end{gathered}
\tag{Problem $p$}
\end{equation}
A mechanism is \emph{optimal} if it solves \eqref{Eqn_Problem_p}.

\subsection{Marginal Revenue}

Let $\overline{p}$ be the lower convex envelope of a feasible price schedule $p$. By convex conjugacy, $p$ and $\overline{p}$ induces the same indirect utility function $u_{p}$. Moreover, the demand set of any bundle $\mathbf{q}\in \mathcal{Q}$ is the subdifferential of $\overline{p}$ at $\mathbf{q}$, defined as
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{D}(\mathbf{q})=\partial \overline{p}(\mathbf{q})=\{\mathbf{x}\in \mathcal{X}: \mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}-\overline{p}(\mathbf{q})\geq \mathbf{x}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{q}}-\overline{p}(\hat{\mathbf{q}})\text{ for all }\hat{\mathbf{q}}\in \mathcal{Q}\}.
\end{equation*}
For any $\mathbf{q}$, $\mathcal{D}(\mathbf{q})$ is a compact and convex subset of $\mathcal{X}$. We extend the notation $\mathcal{D}$ to a set of bundles $Q\subseteq \mathcal{Q}$ by $\mathcal{D}(Q)=\bigcup_{\mathbf{q}\in Q} \mathcal{D}(\mathbf{q})$.

Now we pick an arbitrary $Q$ such that $\mathbf{0}\notin Q$. Consider changing the prices of all bundles in $Q$ by some $\varepsilon$, where $\varepsilon$ may be either positive or negative. The resulting price schedule is $\overline{p}+\varepsilon \mathbb{I}_{Q}$, which induces the following indirect utility function
\begin{equation*}
u_{\overline{p}+\varepsilon \mathbb{I}_{Q}}(\mathbf{x})=\sup_{\mathbf{q}\in \mathcal{Q}}\{\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}-(\overline{p}(\mathbf{q})+ \varepsilon \mathbb{I}_{Q}(\mathbf{q}))\},
\end{equation*}
and total revenue
\begin{equation*}
\TR (\overline{p}+\varepsilon \mathbb{I}_{Q})=\int_{\mathcal{X}}u_{\overline{p}+\varepsilon \mathbb{I}_{Q}}\dd \mu.
\end{equation*}
Whenever the limits exist, we define the one-sided marginal revenue, $\MR_{-}$ and $\MR_{+}$, as
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
\MR_{-}(Q) & =\lim_{\varepsilon\to 0-}\frac{\TR (\overline{p}+\varepsilon \mathbb{I}_{Q})-\TR (p)}{\varepsilon}=\lim_{\varepsilon\to 0-}\int_{\mathcal{X}}\left(\frac{u_{\overline{p}+\varepsilon \mathbb{I}_{Q}}-u_{p}}{\varepsilon}\right)\dd \mu, \\
\MR_{+}(Q) & =\lim_{\varepsilon\to 0+}\frac{\TR (\overline{p}+\varepsilon \mathbb{I}_{Q})-\TR (p)}{\varepsilon}=\lim_{\varepsilon\to 0+}\int_{\mathcal{X}}\left(\frac{u_{\overline{p}+\varepsilon \mathbb{I}_{Q}}-u_{p}}{\varepsilon}\right)\dd \mu.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
If $\MR_{-}(Q)=\MR_{+}(Q)$, we say that $\MR (Q)$ exists and define its value as the one-sided marginal revenue.

\Cref{Lem_MR} fully characterizes $\MR_{-}$ and $\MR_{+}$ and provides a condition for the existence of $\MR$. In its statement, $Q^{c}=\mathcal{Q}\setminus Q$.\footnote{We implicitly assume that $\mathcal{D}(Q)$ and $\mathcal{D}(Q^{c})$ are measurable with respect to $\mu$. If $\mathcal{Q}$ is finite, $\mathcal{D}(Q)$ and $\mathcal{D}(Q^{c})$ are unions of countably many closed (and convex) subsets of $\mathcal{X}$, thus measurability is not an issue. For general $\mathcal{Q}$, this assumption can be justified in an approximate sense.}

\begin{lemma}
\label{Lem_MR}
For any feasible mechanism, if $\mathbf{0}\notin Q$, then
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
\MR_{-}(Q) & =-\mu (\mathcal{D}(Q)), \\
\MR_{+}(Q) & =1+\mu (\mathcal{D}(Q^{c})).
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
Moreover, if $\mu (\mathcal{D}(Q)\cap \mathcal{D}(Q^{c}))=0$, then $\MR (Q)$ exists.
\end{lemma}

When $p$ is optimal, $\TR (\overline{p}+\varepsilon \mathbb{I}_{Q})$ as a function of $\varepsilon$ reaches its global maximum at $\varepsilon=0$, which implies the following two types of local necessary conditions.

First-order conditions (FOCs):
\begin{align}
\label{Eqn_MR-}
\MR_{-}(Q) & \geq 0, \tag{FOC-1} \\
\label{Eqn_MR+}
\MR_{+}(Q) & \leq 0, \tag{FOC-2}
\end{align}
and, when $\mu (\mathcal{D}(Q)\cap \mathcal{D}(Q^{c}))=0$,
\begin{equation}
\label{Eqn_MR}
\MR (Q)=0. \tag{FOC-3}
\end{equation}

Second-order conditions (SOCs): Let $\MR_{-\varepsilon}(Q)=\MR_{-}(Q;\overline{p}+\varepsilon \mathbb{I}_{Q})$ and $\MR_{+\varepsilon}(Q)=\MR_{+}(Q;\overline{p}+\varepsilon \mathbb{I}_{Q})$. For any $\overline{\varepsilon}>0$, there exist $\varepsilon_{1}\in (-\overline{\varepsilon},0)$ and $\varepsilon_{2}\in (0,+\overline{\varepsilon})$, such that
\begin{align}
\label{Eqn_SOC_1}
\MR_{-\varepsilon_{1}}(Q) & \geq 0, \tag{SOC-1} \\
\label{Eqn_SOC_2}
\MR_{+\varepsilon_{1}}(Q) & \geq 0, \tag{SOC-2} \\
\label{Eqn_SOC_3}
\MR_{-\varepsilon_{2}}(Q) & \leq 0, \tag{SOC-3} \\
\label{Eqn_SOC_4}
\MR_{+\varepsilon_{2}}(Q) & \leq 0. \tag{SOC-4}
\end{align}

Clearly, \eqref{Eqn_MR-} and \eqref{Eqn_MR+} follow from the definitions of $\MR_{-}$ and $\MR_{+}$. \eqref{Eqn_MR} follows from \Cref{Lem_MR}. Suppose that \eqref{Eqn_SOC_1} fails. Then there exists $\overline{\varepsilon}>0$ such that $\MR_{-\varepsilon}(Q)<0$ for any $\varepsilon\in (-\overline{\varepsilon},0)$. By \citet[Theorem 1]{miller1986some}, $\TR (\overline{p}+\varepsilon\mathbb{I}_{Q})$ is strictly decreasing on $(-\overline{\varepsilon},0)$, a contradiction to the optimality of $p$.\footnote{Strictly speaking, the result of \citet{miller1986some} only ensures that $\TR (\overline{p}+\varepsilon\mathbb{I}_{Q})$ is nonincreasing if $\MR_{-\varepsilon}(Q)\leq 0$ for any $\varepsilon\in (-\overline{\varepsilon},0)$. Since we have $\MR_{-\varepsilon}(Q)<0$, it is straightforward to stengthen their result to strict monotonicity.} \eqref{Eqn_SOC_2}-\eqref{Eqn_SOC_4} can be proved analogously.

Our main result, \Cref{Thm_MR}, is simply a formal statement of these necessary conditions.

\begin{theorem}
\label{Thm_MR}
For any optimal mechanism, if $\mathbf{0}\notin Q$, then \eqref{Eqn_MR-}-\eqref{Eqn_MR} and \eqref{Eqn_SOC_1}-\eqref{Eqn_SOC_4} hold.
\end{theorem}

The qualification $\mu (\mathcal{D}(Q)\cap \mathcal{D}(Q^{c}))=0$ for \eqref{Eqn_MR} cannot be omitted. For instance, when $N=2$, $\mathbf{x}$ is uniformly distributed over $\mathcal{X}=[\underline{x},\underline{x}+1]^2$ for some $\underline{x}>0$, and $\mathcal{Q}=[0,1]^2$, \citet{pavlov2011optimal} has proved that the optimal mechanism entails randomization when $\underline{x}$ is small and becomes pure bundling when $\underline{x}$ is large. It can also be verified that $\mathbf{e}_{1}$ and $\mathbf{e}_{2}$ are both inactive. In fact, as depicted by the blue lines in \Cref{Fig_Uniform_Positive}, $\mathcal{D}(\mathbf{e}_{2})$ is a zero measure set on the boundary $\{\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{X}:x_{1}=0\}$. By \Cref{Lem_MR}, $\MR_{-}(\mathbf{e}_{2})=-\mu (\mathcal{D}(\mathbf{e}_{2}))>0$, meaning that \eqref{Eqn_MR} does not hold.\footnote{This counterexample disproves a claim made by \citet{kash2016optimal} that $\mu (\mathcal{D}(\mathbf{q}))=0$ for any $\mathbf{q}\in \mathcal{Q}$.}

