For the general case where $\mathcal{X}=[\underline{x},\overline{x}]^{N}$, we say that $\overline{\mathbf{q}}$ is the \emph{grand bundle} if $\overline{\mathbf{x}}\cdot \overline{\mathbf{q}}> \overline{\mathbf{x}}\cdot \mathbf{q}$ for all $\mathbf{q}\in \mathcal{Q}$. That is, $\overline{\mathbf{q}}$ is strictly preferred by the ``top'' buyers. Since $(1/\sqrt{N})\mathbf{1}\in \Int \NC (\overline{\mathbf{q}})$, $\overline{\mathbf{q}}$ is pooling in any optimal mechanism.

\begin{corollary}
\label{Cor_Grandbundle}
Suppose that $\mathcal{X}=[\underline{x},\overline{x}]^{N}$ and that the grand bundle $\overline{\mathbf{q}}$ exists. Then in any optimal mechanism, $\overline{\mathbf{q}}$ is pooling.
\end{corollary}

If $\mathcal{Q}=[0,1]^{N}$, then $\mathbf{1}$ is the grand bundle. In this setting, \Cref{Cor_Grandbundle} generalizes Theorem 16 of \citet{manelli2007multidimensional}, which shows that $\mathbf{1}$ is pooling in any \emph{piecewise linear} optimal mechanism.

