\section{Concluding Remarks}
\label{Sec_Conclusion}

In this paper, we demonstrate that FOC and SOC based on price perturbations pin down how optimal selling mechanisms factor in distributional information. For SRS distributions, the approach characterizes the optimal mechanisms, and depending on the shape of $\zeta$, they feature pure bundling, mixed bundling, and probabilistic bundling with either finite or infinite menu sizes.\footnote{Since $\zeta$ facilitates the reduction of a two-dimensional problem to that of finding a one-dimensional function $\overline{u}$, we conjecture that this approach can be applied recursively to derive explicit solutions in higher-dimensional strictly regular distributions, such as the example of selling $N$ uniformly distributed items in \citet{giannakopoulos2018duality}.} As a result, we illustrate how a multiproduct monopolist accounts for multidimensional characteristics such as correlation and substitution or complementarity. Conversely, our approach also nails down necessary/sufficient conditions on the type distribution for a specific selling mechanism to be optimal. Moreover, FOC and SOC lead to novel insights on active as well as inactive bundles for general distributions. 

Our paper complements the recent literature on optimal informationally robust selling mechanisms with multiple items. For instance, \citet{carroll2017robustness}, \citet{deb2023multi}, and \citet{che2024robustly} solve optimal multiproduct mechanisms that maximize the seller's worst-case revenue over information structures subject to marginal, moment, and MPC constraints, respectively. As opposed to leveraging the distributional information, these papers appeal to a saddle-point argument via finding a minmax information structure and a maxmin mechanism whose optimal revenue coincide. In contrast, our result demonstrates how the buyer's type distribution dictates optimal selling mechanisms such as whether and how to randomize/bundle. Comparing our Bayesian optimal revenue with the optimal revenue guarantees identifies the value of distributional information beyond moments or marginal distributions.

Second, while we focus on monopoly pricing, the MR approach may also shed light on oligopolistic competition with firms selling multiple products. See, for example, recent contributions by \citet{armstrong2010competitive,zhou2017competitive}, and \citet{zhou2021mixed} on competitive (pure or mixed) bundling. As taxation mechanisms offer a general formulation of nonlinear pricing that allows for flexible bundling choice, our analysis calls for a similar extension to an oligopoly market. Moreover, contrasting optimal revenues of a multiproduct monopoly and oligopolistic firms selling each product separately measures the synergy of a merger.

Finally, a refined understanding of the leading case of multiproduct monopoly calls for similar investigation of other multidimensional screening problems by means of FOC and SOC such as delegation (e.g., \citet{kleiner2022optimal}) and persuasion with privately informed receivers (e.g., \citet{kolotilin2017persuasion}). Moreover, the MR approach may also be useful in characterizing optimal mechanisms for single-dimensional screening problems that require randomization, including costly state verification (e.g., \citet{mookherjee1989optimal}) and adverse selection with type-dependent participation constraints (e.g., \citet{jullien2000participation}). \citet{chen2025} have attempted to characterize optimal random audit mechanisms and optimal random delegation mechanisms. 

