\subsection{Verifying the Optimality of Specific Mechanisms for Selling Two Products}
\label{Sec_Verify_2D}

Starting from \citet{adams1976commodity}, there has been extensive discussion on the optimality of several bundling strategies commonly observed in practice, namely:
\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Separate selling}: The set of active bundles is either $\{\mathbf{e}_{1},\mathbf{e}_{2}\}$ or $\{\mathbf{e}_{1},\mathbf{e}_{2},\mathbf{1}\}$ with $p(\mathbf{1})=p(\mathbf{e}_{1})+p(\mathbf{e}_{2})$.
\item \emph{Mixed bundling}: The set of active bundles is $\{\mathbf{e}_{1},\mathbf{e}_{2},\mathbf{1}\}$ with $p(\mathbf{1})<p(\mathbf{e}_{1})+p(\mathbf{e}_{2})$.
\item \emph{Pure bundling}: The only active bundle is $\mathbf{1}$.
\end{itemize}
Much of the early literature, such as \citet{mcafee1989multiproduct}, focuses on a partial comparison of several bundling strategies, primarily due to the difficulty of analyzing stochastic mechanisms. With Propositions \ref{Prop_2D} and \ref{Prop_SubsComp}, we can address this question from a broader perspective, that is, identifying the conditions under which a bundling strategy is optimal among all feasible mechanisms. The settings are the same as those in \Cref{Sec_SubsComp}, and when $k=1$ we are back to \Cref{Sec_2D}.

We begin by introducing two conditions for pure bundling for $k=1$. Unlike corollaries in \Cref{Sec_2D}, \Cref{Cor_Pure} assumes the existence of a revenue-maximizing pure bundling mechanism and links its optimality to the shape of $\zeta$.

\begin{corollary}
\label{Cor_Pure}
Suppose that $k=1$ and that $p$ is a revenue-maximizing pure bundling mechanism. Then
\begin{enumerate}
\item $p$ is optimal only if \label{Cor_Pure_1}
\begin{equation}
\label{Eqn_Pure_Nec}
p(\mathbf{1})\leq \zeta (0).
\end{equation}
\item Suppose that $\zeta$ crosses the 45-degree line exactly once. Then $p$ is optimal if, for any $x\in [0,1]$, \label{Cor_Pure_2}
\begin{equation}
\label{Eqn_Pure_Suf}
p(\mathbf{1})-x\leq \zeta (x).
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Part \ref{Cor_Pure_1} follows from \Cref{Prop_2D}, so we will only prove part \ref{Cor_Pure_2}.
By \Cref{Sec_2D_Finite}, $p$ should satisfy $\MR (\mathbf{1};p)=0$. Suppose that $\hat{p}$ is optimal and is not a pure bundling mechanism. Let $\hat{u}$ be the indirect utility function induced by $\hat{p}$. We will discuss two cases, each illustrated in a separate panel of \Cref{Fig_Pure}, where the notations follow the same conventions as \Cref{Fig_Mixed}. That $\zeta$ crosses the 45-degree line exactly once ensures $\zeta(x)>x$ for all $x\in [0,a^{1}]$, where $a^{1}$ is specified with respect to $\hat{p}$.

Panel (a) depicts the first case where $\hat{p}(\mathbf{1})>p(\mathbf{1})$, which implies $\mathcal{D}(\mathbf{1};\hat{p})\subset \mathcal{D}(\mathbf{1};p)$ and $\MR (\mathbf{1};\hat{p})<\MR (\mathbf{1};p)=0$ by \eqref{Eqn_Pure_Suf}, a contradiction to \Cref{Prop_2D}. Panel (b) shows the second case where $\hat{p}(\mathbf{1})\leq p(\mathbf{1})$, which implies $1-\hat{u}(x,1)<1-u(x,1)\leq \zeta (x)$ for all $x\in [0,a^{1}]$. In other words, $\max (1-\hat{u}(x,1),x)<\zeta (x)$ for all $[0,a^{1}]$, meaning that no active bundle of the form $(q,1)$ with $q<1$ can satisfy parts \ref{Prop_2D_2} or \ref{Prop_2D_3} of \Cref{Prop_2D}.

Hence, our supposition is not true, and $p$ is optimal.
\end{proof}

