\centering
\input{Fig_Pure}
\caption{Pure bundling is optimal.}
\label{Fig_Pure}
\end{figure}

The following result is a direct consequence of \Cref{Cor_Pure}: Suppose that $\zeta$ is nondecreasing or concave. Then a revenue-maximizing pure bundling mechanism $p$ is optimal if and only if \eqref{Eqn_Pure_Nec} holds.

In a similar two-product setting, \citet{menicucci2015optimality} provide sufficient conditions for the optimality of pure bundling, but their conditions rely on the assumption that the type space is bounded away from the origin. In this sense, \Cref{Cor_Pure} complements their results. \citet{haghpanah2021pure} and \citet{ghili2023characterization} also study pure bundling without restricting the number of products. While their modeling approach differs from ours in \Cref{Sec_2D}, it shares many similarities with the setting discussed in \Cref{Sec_SubsComp}.

We can apply a similar approach to study the optimality of deterministic mechanisms that cover both mixed bundling and pure bundling.

\begin{corollary}
\label{Cor_Mixed}
Suppose that $k=1$ and that $p$ is a revenue-maximizing deterministic mechanism. Then
\begin{enumerate}
\item $p$ is optimal only if \label{Cor_Mixed_1}
\begin{equation}
\label{Eqn_Mixed}
p(\mathbf{e}_{2})\leq \zeta (p(\mathbf{1})-p(\mathbf{e}_{2})),
\end{equation}
where we let $p(\mathbf{e}_{2})=p(\mathbf{1})$ if $\mathbf{e}_{2}$ is inactive.
\item Suppose that $\zeta$ is concave. Then the converse is also true, that is, $p$ is optimal if and only if \eqref{Eqn_Mixed} holds. \label{Cor_Mixed_2}
\end{enumerate}
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof} 
Part \ref{Cor_Mixed_1} follows from \Cref{Prop_2D}, so we will only prove part \ref{Cor_Mixed_2}. Without loss of generality, we assume that $\mathbf{e}_{2}$ is active under $p$. The proof is mainly geometric and makes use of \Cref{Fig_Mixed}.

By \Cref{Sec_2D_Finite}, $p$ should satisfy $\MR (\mathbf{e}_{2};p)=\MR (\mathbf{1};p)=0$. Suppose that $\hat{p}$ is optimal and involves randomization. Let $\hat{u}$ be the indirect utility function induced by $\hat{p}$. By \Cref{Cor_Ccv}, there exist $0\leq b^{0}<a^{1}\leq 1$ such that $\overline{u}(x)=1-\zeta (x)$ on $[b^{0},a^{1})$. We will discuss three cases, each illustrated in a separate panel of \Cref{Fig_Mixed}. In each panel, $\zeta$ is shown in green, $1-u(x,1)$ (or $1-\hat{u}(x,1)$) and its symmetric counterpart below the 45-degree line are represented by red (or cyan) curves, and demand sets are separated by dashed lines.

Panel (a) depicts the first case where $\hat{p}(\mathbf{e}_{2})>p(\mathbf{e}_{2})$ and $\hat{p}(\mathbf{1})>p(\mathbf{1})$. Since $p$ satisfies \eqref{Eqn_Mixed}, we have $\hat{u}(x,1)<u(x,1)$ for all $x\in [0,1]$, meaning that $\MR (\mathbf{e}_{2};\hat{p})+\MR (\mathbf{1};\hat{p})<\MR (\mathbf{e}_{2};p)+\MR (\mathbf{1};p)=0$. Panel (b) shows the second case where $\hat{p}(\mathbf{e}_{2})\leq p(\mathbf{e}_{2})$ and $\hat{p}(\mathbf{1})>p(\mathbf{1})$. In this case, $\mathcal{D}(\mathbf{1};\hat{p})\subset \mathcal{D}(\mathbf{1};p)$, which implies $\MR (\mathbf{1};\hat{p})<\MR (\mathbf{1};p)=0$ by \eqref{Eqn_Mixed}. Panel (c) illustrates the third case where $\hat{p}(\mathbf{e}_{2})>p(\mathbf{e}_{2})$ and $\hat{p}(\mathbf{1})\leq p(\mathbf{1})$. By a similar argument, there are $\mathcal{D}(\mathbf{1};p)\subset \mathcal{D}(\mathbf{1};\hat{p})$ and $\MR (\mathbf{1};\hat{p})>\MR (\mathbf{1};p)=0$. Therefore, all three cases lead to a contradiction to \Cref{Prop_2D}. The red shaded area in each panel illustrates the difference in MR surplus between $\hat{p}$ and $p$. 

Finally, note that $\hat{p}(\mathbf{e}_{2})\leq p(\mathbf{e}_{2})$ and $\hat{p}(\mathbf{1})\leq p(\mathbf{1})$ cannot hold simultaneously due to \eqref{Eqn_Mixed}. Hence, our supposition is not true, and $p$ is optimal.
\end{proof}

