\label{Fig_Mixed}
\end{figure}

Recall that \Cref{Cor_Inc} establishes that optimal mechanisms are deterministic when $\zeta$ is nondecreasing. \Cref{Cor_Mixed} can be understood as an extension of \Cref{Cor_Inc} to other shapes of $\zeta$. It complements a large body of existing work that seeks sufficient conditions to eliminate randomization, such as \citet{mcafee1988multidimensional,manelli2006bundling}, and \citet{hart2010revenue}.

When items are perfect substitutes, making the grand bundle active is clearly not optimal. Instead, we have two conditions for the optimality of a separate selling mechanism.

\begin{corollary}
\label{Cor_Separate}
Suppose that $k=1/2$ and that $p$ is a revenue-maximizing separate selling mechanism. Then
\begin{enumerate}
\item $p$ is optimal only if \label{Cor_Separate_1}
\begin{equation}
\label{Eqn_Separate_1}
p(\mathbf{e}_{2})\leq \zeta_{k}(1).
\end{equation}
\item Suppose there exists $x_{\zeta}\in [0,1]$ such that $\zeta_{k}(x)=0$ for $x\in [0,x_{\zeta})$. Then $p$ is optimal if, for any $x\in [x_{\zeta},1]$, \label{Cor_Separate_2}
\begin{equation}
\label{Eqn_Separate_2}
p(\mathbf{e}_{2})\leq \zeta_{k} (x).
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Part \ref{Cor_Separate_1} follows from \Cref{Prop_SubsComp}, so we will only prove part \ref{Cor_Separate_2}. Similar to the proofs of Corollaries \ref{Cor_Mixed} and \ref{Cor_Pure}, $p$ should satisfy $\MR (\mathbf{e}_{2};p)=0$. Suppose that $\hat{p}$ is optimal and is not a separate selling mechanism. Let $\hat{u}$ be the indirect utility function induced by $\hat{p}$. We will discuss two cases, each illustrated in a separate panel of \Cref{Fig_Pure}, where the notations follow the same conventions as \Cref{Fig_Mixed}.

Panel (a) depicts the first case where $\hat{p}(\mathbf{e}_{2})>p(\mathbf{e}_{2})$. In this case, $\mathcal{D}(\mathbf{e}_{2};\hat{p})\subset \mathcal{D}(\mathbf{e}_{2};p)$, which, by \eqref{Eqn_Separate_2}, implies that $\MR (\mathbf{e}_{2};\hat{p})<\MR (\mathbf{e}_{2};p)=0$, a contradiction to \Cref{Prop_2D}. Panel (b) shows the second case where $\hat{p}(\mathbf{e}_{2})\leq p(\mathbf{e}_{2})$. In this case, $1-\hat{u}(x,1)$ is bounded away from $\zeta$ by $1-u(x,1)$, meaning that no active bundle of the form $(q,1-q)$ with $q>0$ can satisfy parts \ref{Prop_SubsComp_2} or \ref{Prop_SubsComp_3} of \Cref{Prop_SubsComp}.
\end{proof}

