\begin{abstract}
Safe trajectory planning remains a significant challenge in complex environments, where traditional methods often trade off computational efficiency for safety. Comprehensive obstacle modeling improves safety but is computationally expensive, while approximate methods are more efficient but may compromise safety. To address this issue, this paper introduces a rapid and safe trajectory planning framework based on state-based diffusion models. Leveraging only low-dimensional vehicle states, the diffusion models achieve notable inference efficiency while ensuring sufficient collision-free characteristics. By composing diffusion models, the proposed framework can safely generalize across diverse scenarios, planning collision-free trajectories even in unseen scenes. To further ensure the safety of the generated trajectories, an efficient, rule-based safety filter is proposed, which selects optimal trajectories that satisfy both sufficient safety and control feasibility from among candidate trajectories. Both in seen and unseen scenarios, the proposed method achieves efficient inference time while maintaining high safety and stability. Evaluations on the F1TENTH vehicle further demonstrate that the proposed method is practical in real-world applications. The project page is at: \textcolor{blue}{ \url{ https://rstp-comp-diffuser.github.io/}}.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
	Diffusion Model, Composition, Safety, Trajectory Planning, Generalization
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}


\IEEEPARstart{T}{rajectory} planning in complex scenes is typically divided into two phases: initial path generation and optimization-based trajectory optimization. Initial collision-free paths can be generated quickly using search or sampling-based methods~\cite{dolgov2008practical}. However, these methods require significant computational time in complex scenarios~\cite{zhang2020optimization} and often produce paths with insufficient control feasibility~\cite{liRapidIterativeTrajectory}. As a result, optimization-based planning becomes essential to ensure that the planned trajectory is both control-feasible and collision-free, which makes the overall planning process time-consuming. In summary, balancing computational efficiency, control feasibility, and collision avoidance is a challenging yet crucial task for ensuring vehicle safety.

Imitation learning is regarded as an effective solution to this problem, as it can leverage optimization-generated trajectories as expert demonstrations to learn the distribution of optimal trajectories~\cite{urain2024deep}. Among imitation learning methods, the diffusion model is widely used due to its ease of training~\cite{janner2022planning}, stable generation process~\cite{ho2020denoising}, and support for multi-modal inputs~\cite{chi2023diffusion}. However, the diffusion model suffers from slow trajectory generation due to its iterative denoising process, which can lead to failure in real-world deployment. To address this challenge, we introduce a fast trajectory‐synthesis framework that seamlessly fuses real‐time perception with diffusion‐based generative models. By distilling rich sensor inputs into compact vehicle‐state embeddings, our approach dramatically accelerates the iterative denoising process while fully preserving the diffusion model's capacity to produce precise, high‐fidelity trajectories.

At the same time, traditional global trajectory planning methods exhibit significant limitations in handling diverse scenarios, requiring substantial adjustments, such as modifications to initial paths and planner parameters. To overcome this limitation, we introduce a rapid, diffusion‐based approach for collision-free trajectory planning that seamlessly adapts to diverse scenarios. It leverages diffusion composition to ensure safe generalization.  
Specifically, Figures \ref{fig:vis_abstract} (a) and (b) show diffusion models trained independently on static and dynamic scenes, respectively. By incorporating the dynamic obstacle from scene (b) into the static scene of (a), a new composite scenario is formed. Neither of these models has encountered similar scenarios during training. But individual diffusion models can be composed to generate safe trajectories in this unseen scene, as shown in Figure \ref{fig:vis_abstract} (c). Our approach enables optimal test-time decision-making to produce safe behaviors, such as accelerating to bypass or decelerating to avoid obstacles. This allows safe trajectory planning for diverse scenes without requiring extra adjustments.

We validate the proposed method on the popular F1TENTH platform~\cite{betz2022autonomous}, which is an efficient testbed for planning methods that integrates perception, planning, and control in a unified hardware system. Experiments on a custom F1TENTH vehicle confirm the superior real-world performance of our method.

In summary, the proposed \textbf{R}apid and \textbf{S}afe \textbf{T}rajectory \textbf{P}lanning (RSTP) framework based on diffusion models has the following contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
	\item \textbf{Rapid Trajectory Planning with  Strong Control Feasibility:} The computational load of the denoising process is reduced by using low-dimensional vehicle states from a perception module, reducing the mean computation time of planning to \textbf{0.21s} in consumer-level hardware. Moreover, a dataset generation method is proposed, using model predictive control (MPC) for trajectory tracking. It creates high-quality trajectory datasets as expert demonstrations for diffusion training.

	\item \textbf{Safe Trajectory Planning over Diverse Scenes through Diffusion Composition:} Individual models are composed to plan collision-free trajectories in unseen scenarios, addressing the generalization limitations of imitation learning. Additionally, a rule-based safety filter is proposed to select the safest and most feasible trajectory from the candidate trajectory batch generated by diffusion models, which reduces the collision rate by \textbf{3.07\%} with a minor time increment. 

	\item \textbf{Validation on a Real-World Scaled F1Tenth Vehicle Platform:} The proposed method is validated on an F1TENTH vehicle by combining server-based inference with onboard tracking. The simple pure pursuit controller is used to track the planned trajectory, where the F1TENTH vehicle can accurately execute the control inputs without collision, demonstrating strong control feasibility of our planned trajectory and its applicability to real-world scenarios.
\end{enumerate}

This paper is organized as follows: Section \ref{sec:Preliminaries} introduces the use of diffusion models to represent the planning problem. Section \ref{sec:Method} provides a detailed description of the dataset generation process, the composition of diffusion models for diverse scenes, and the safety filter. Section \ref{sec:Exp} presents numerical simulations and experiments based on the F1TENTH platform. Finally, Section \ref{sec:Conclusion} concludes the paper by summarizing the key findings and outlining directions for future research.



\section{Preliminaries}\label{sec:Preliminaries}

In this section, the method of path planning using potential fields~\cite{lin2022model} and its drawbacks are first introduced. Then, the approach of using Energy-based Models (EBMs) for potential field modeling is discussed in ~\cite{luo2024potential}. Finally, the relationship between diffusion models and EBMs is explored.

\subsection{Path Planning using Potential Field}
Collision-free path planning can be achieved using the potential-based approach~\cite{luo2024potential}. In this method, a potential function $ \mathcal{U}(\boldsymbol{\tau}): \mathbb{R}^{n \times L} \to \mathbb{R} $ is used to assign a potential value to a path $ \boldsymbol{\tau} $, based on predefined attractive and repulsive potentials. Here, $n$ represents the state dimension and $ L$ is the path horizon. Given an initial path $ \boldsymbol{\tau} $, the collision-free path can be derived by applying gradient descent to the potential function as follows:
$
	\boldsymbol{\tau}^k_{1:L} = \boldsymbol{\tau}^{k-1}_{1:L} - \lambda \nabla_{\boldsymbol{\tau}_{1:L}^k} \mathcal{U}(\boldsymbol{\tau}_{1:L}^k),
$
where $ \boldsymbol{\tau}^k_{1:L} $ is the path at step $ k $, and $ \boldsymbol{\tau}^{k-1}_{1:L} $ is the path at the previous step, and $ \lambda $ is the step size for an iteration. However, using fixed potential functions can lead to corner cases, which makes it challenging to apply this method in more complex scenarios.



\subsection{Energy-based Models}

Energy-based Models are a class of generative models~\cite{du2019implicit}. They can serve as surrogate models for the potential function, represented by $\mathcal{U}(\tau) \sim E_\theta (\boldsymbol{\tau})$, addressing the limitations of conventional potential-based methods. Given a path variable $ \boldsymbol{\tau}  $, the probability density $p_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{\tau})$ of a collision-free path $ \boldsymbol{\tau} $ is defined as: $p_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{\tau}) \propto e^{-E_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{\tau})}$, where $ E_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{\tau}): \mathbb{R}^{n \times L } \to \mathbb{R} $ is an energy-based model, typically implemented using a U-Net architecture.  Thus, the path planning process can be modified as follows:
$
	\boldsymbol{\tau}_{1:L}^{t-1} = \boldsymbol{\tau}_{1:L}^{t} - \lambda \nabla_{\boldsymbol{\tau}_{1:L}^t}E_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{\tau}_{1:L}^t,t, \mathcal{C}  )
	+ \eta,
$
where $ \eta \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, {\sigma}_t^2 \boldsymbol{I}) $, with $ \lambda $ and $ \sigma_t $ being  specific scaling values. $t$ is the denoising step. And $\mathcal{C}= \{ \mathbf{s}_s, \mathbf{s}_g, \mathcal{O} \}$ is the set of constraints, which includes the start state and goal state constraints denoted as $\mathbf{s}_s$ and $\mathbf{s}_g$, as well as the obstacle pose constraints $\mathcal{O}$. 	In this paper, $\tau = [ \mathbf{s}_1, \cdots,  \mathbf{s}_{L}]_{4\times L} $ denotes a discrete planned path. Specifically, $\mathbf{s} = [x,\,y,\,q_{z},\,q_{w}]^{\top}$ with $[x,\, y]^{\top}$ representing the Cartesian position and $[q_{z},\,q_{w}]^{\top}$ the components of the quaternion encoding the  yaw angle.

\subsection{Diffusion Models}
Diffusion models $\epsilon_{\theta}$ effectively estimate the energy-based model’s gradient, denote as $\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\tau}^t_{1:L}} E_{\theta} (\boldsymbol{\tau}^t_{1:L}, \mathcal{C}) \propto   \epsilon_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{\tau}^t_{1:L}, t,  \mathcal{C})$. The loss function for a single diffusion model training is:
\begin{equation*}
	\label{eq:Loss}
	\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{MSE}}=
	\Vert  \epsilon -
	\epsilon_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{\tau}^t_{1:L}, t,  \mathcal{C}
	)\Vert^{2}
\end{equation*}
where $\epsilon \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, \boldsymbol{I})$ represents Gaussian noise.  $\boldsymbol{\tau}_{1:L}^t$ is the generated path at the denoising step $t$.


The forward noise addition process is given by:
$q(\boldsymbol{\tau}_{1:L}^{t} \mid \boldsymbol{\tau}_{1:L}^{t-1}) := \mathcal{N}(\sqrt{1 - \beta_t} \boldsymbol{\tau}_{1:L}^t, \, \beta_t \boldsymbol{I}),$
where $\beta_t$ is the variance schedule. After a sufficient number of noising steps $T$, the final distribution of $q(\boldsymbol{\tau}^{\top}_{1:L})$ converges to:
$q(\boldsymbol{\tau}^{\top}_{1:L}) \approx \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, \boldsymbol{I}).$
On the other hand, the reverse denoising process can be expressed as:
$p_\theta(\boldsymbol{\tau}_{1:L}^{t-1} \mid \boldsymbol{\tau}_{1:L}^t) := \mathcal{N}(\mu_\theta(\boldsymbol{\tau}_{1:L}^t, t), \, \tilde{\beta_t}^2 \boldsymbol{I}),$
where
$\mu_\theta(\boldsymbol{\tau}_{1:L}^t, t) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_t}} \left( \boldsymbol{\tau}_{1:L}^t - \frac{\beta_t}{\sqrt{1 - \bar{\alpha}_t}} \epsilon_\theta(\boldsymbol{\tau}_{1:L}^t, t) \right),$
and $\alpha_t, \bar{\alpha}_t, \tilde{\beta_t}$ are functions of $\beta_t$.


\section{The Proposed Rapid and Safe Trajectory Planning Method} \label{sec:Method}

Our approach proceeds in three stages. First, we describe a comprehensive dataset‐generation procedure. Second, we achieve generalization to unseen scenes by composing the individually trained diffusion models. Finally, a safety filter is applied to guarantee the safety of the final planned trajectory. The entire pipeline is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:pipeline}.

\vspace{-5pt}
\subsection{Datasets Generation}\label{section:dataset_generation}

For collision avoidance in the static scene, global collision-free paths are generated using the iterative collision avoidance (ItCA) method~\cite{liRapidIterativeTrajectory}. The ItCA path planning accounts for the smoothness of gear shifting points (GSPs), ensuring the control feasibility of planned paths. Then, an MPC planner is employed for path tracking to produce the final global trajectory. The entire trajectory enriches the static dataset, implicitly encoding velocity through the density of trajectory points within a fixed interval $\Delta t$. Implicit learning of velocity enables the planning of more complex trajectories, exhibiting various behaviors for collision avoidance, such as decelerating to yield or accelerating to bypass.

The vehicle model $f(\cdot)$ used for MPC is the kinematic model employed in~\cite{zhang2020optimization}. The state is represented as $\boldsymbol{\zeta} = [x,\, y,\, \varphi,\, v]^\top$ and the control input as $\mathbf{u} = [\delta,  \,a]^\top$. $\delta$ is the steering angle, and $a$ represents acceleration. $v$ is the velocity and $\varphi$ represents the vehicle heading.

\begin{figure}[!t]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=3.0in]{media/potential.pdf}
	\caption{ \textbf{Diffusion Composition.} Trajectory planning using the energy model as a surrogate model for the potential field.}
	\label{fig:potential}
    \vspace{-10pt}
\end{figure}

For global collision-free ItCA path planning, starting states $\mathbf{s}_{s}$  are arbitrarily sampled within the feasible region as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline}. The goal states $\mathbf{s}_{g}$ are fixed for all candidate points. The velocity of both $\mathbf{s}_{s}$ and $\mathbf{s}_{g}$ are 0. The reference path pose sequence generated by the ItCA method, $\mathbf{p}_{s_k}^{\text{ref}} = [x_{s_k}^{\text{ref}},\, y_{s_k}^{\text{ref}},\, \varphi_{s_k}^{\text{ref}}]^\top$, is parameterized by the arc length $s_k$.

In dynamic scenes, a penalty term based on the distance between the ego vehicle and dynamic obstacles is added to the MPC planner's cost function to promote collision avoidance:
$
	J_\textrm{dynamic} = \alpha \sum_{i=1}^{N_{\mathrm{o}}} ({\Vert \mathbf{p}_{\text{cur}}-\mathbf{o}_i \Vert_2})^{-1}
  $, where $N_o$ is the number of dynamic obstacles and $\mathbf{o}_i$ is the pose of the $i$-th obstacle. $\alpha$ is the coefficient. $\mathbf{p}_{\text{cur}}$ is the current vehicle pose. It is emphasized that dynamic obstacle avoidance does not consider static obstacles, which may result in collisions with them in the planned trajectory.

 The improved Euler method is used to discretize the kinematic model $f(\boldsymbol{\zeta})$, where $\boldsymbol{\zeta}_0$ represents the current state of the vehicle. Then the optimization problem for the MPC planner across diverse scenes is expressed as follows:
\begin{equation}
	\begin{aligned}\label{eq:mpc}
		\min_{\boldsymbol{\zeta}, \mathbf{u}} \quad & \gamma \cdot J_\textrm{dynamic} + (1-\gamma) \cdot \Big{(} \sum_{k=1}^{N_p}   \overbrace{ \left( \Vert \mathbf{A} \cdot \boldsymbol{\zeta}_k - \mathbf{p}_{s_k}^{\text{ref}} \Vert^2_{Q_1} \right)}^{J_\textrm{static}}  \\
		                                            & + \sum_{k=1}^{N_p-1}\underbrace{ \Vert \Delta \mathbf{u}_k \Vert^2_{R_1} + \Vert \mathbf{A} \cdot \boldsymbol{\zeta}_{N_p} - \mathbf{p}_{s_{N_p}}^{\text{ref}} \Vert^2_{Q_2} + \Vert \mathbf{u}_k \Vert^2_{R_2}   \Big{)} }_{J_\textrm{static}} \\
		\text{s.t.} \quad
		                                            & \boldsymbol{\zeta}_{k} = \boldsymbol{\zeta}_{k-1} + T_s \cdot f \left( \boldsymbol{\zeta}_k + \frac{T_s}{2} f \left( \boldsymbol{\zeta}_k, \mathbf{u}_k \right), \mathbf{u}_k \right), \\
		                                            & \boldsymbol{\zeta}_0 = \boldsymbol{\zeta}_{\textrm{cur}}, \quad \boldsymbol{\zeta}_{\textrm{min}} \leq \lvert \boldsymbol{\zeta}_k \rvert \leq \boldsymbol{\zeta}_{\textrm{max}}, \quad \mathbf{u}_{\textrm{min}} \leq \lvert \mathbf{u}_k \rvert \leq \mathbf{u}_{\textrm{max}},
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
\looseness=-1
where $\mathbf{A} = [1,\, 1,\, 1,\, 0]^\top$, and $N_p$ denotes the prediction horizon. $\boldsymbol{\zeta}_{\textrm{cur}}$ is the current vehicle state. In dynamic scenes, $\gamma = 1$; otherwise, $\gamma = 0$.  Following the concept of projected velocity in~\cite{li2024data,li2025reduce}, the value of $s_k$ is determined by a fixed projection velocity $v_{\text{ref}}$, such that $s_k = s_{k-1} + \Delta t \cdot v_{\text{ref}}$, with $s_0 = s_{\text{cur}}$. $\boldsymbol{\zeta}_{\text{max}}$ and $\boldsymbol{\zeta}_{\text{min}}$ denote the upper and lower bounds of the state constraints, while $\mathbf{u}_{\text{max}}$ and $\mathbf{u}_{\text{min}}$ are the upper and lower limits of the control inputs, respectively. $T_s$ represents the prediction interval.  $Q_1$, $Q_2$, $R_1$, and $R_2$ are coefficient matrices.

