\subsection{Evaluate Compositional Performance in Real-world}
The real-world experiments are conducted using a self-built F1TENTH vehicle. For mapping and localization, the vehicle utilizes the Cartographer ROS framework~\cite{hess2016real}. Communication between the server and onboard computer is managed via the Message Queuing Telemetry Transport (MQTT)  protocol. The F1TENTH vehicle uses the pure pursuit controller proposed in~\cite{weng2024aggressive} to track trajectories. The RSTP method can plan sufficiently safe trajectories in the $SS$ as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:static_exp}.

The effectiveness of the model composition for safety is first validated through trajectory planning in real-world scenarios $CS_1$ and $CS_2$. In scenario $CS_1$, the velocities corresponding to trajectories planned by different models are depicted in Fig.~\ref{fig:real_v}. The trajectory generated by the DyM1 alone results in a collision with a static obstacle. However, when composing the StM with the DyM1, the StM steers the planned trajectory of DyM1, effectively avoiding collisions with static obstacles. This generalization capability is clearly evident from the decision to decelerate, as marked in Fig.~\ref{fig:real_v}. Similarly, in scenario $CS_2$ as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:exp_compose2}, the composed model successfully generates a collision-free trajectory. These experimental results demonstrate that the model composition enhances the safety of the RSTP method for real-world applications.

To further evaluate the safety of the proposed RSTP method, three distinct starting points are selected within obstacle-free regions of the map in both scenes $CS_1$ and $CS_2$. The safety metric used is the minimum distance between the vehicle vertices and both static and dynamic obstacles, with results summarized in Table \ref{tab:real_comp}. Even with localization uncertainty, the minimum obstacle distance recorded is \textbf{0.032 m}, confirming adequate safety margins. Moreover, fewer than 10\% of trajectory points fall within the defined danger threshold, indicating that only a minor segment of the planned trajectory poses a potential collision risk. Thus, the composed model proves to be sufficiently safe in the context of real-world perception uncertainty.

