	\subsection{Experiment Setup}\label{app:sub1}

	To evaluate the RSTP, training and evaluation datasets are constructed from a real-world $6\,\text{m} \times 6\,\text{m}$ map. From the map's free navigable area, a comprehensive evaluation dataset $D_A$ containing $N_D^\text{all} = 1045$ uniformly sampled initial poses is created. To specifically assess the compositional model, a subset $D_C$ with $N_D^\text{comp} = 79$ initial poses is selected from $D_A$. In the individual evaluation of the diffusion model, target poses remain fixed. The trajectory horizon $L$ is set to 128 with 100 diffusion steps during training. Denoising employs the DDIM method with 8 steps, and the number of candidate trajectories $N_{\text{filter}}$ for the safety filter is set to 8. Model training and inference are performed using an RTX 4060 GPU and a 4.9 GHz Intel(R) i7-12700 CPU with 48GB RAM.
     \vspace{-10pt}
    \subsection{ On the Control Feasibility of our \text{RSTP} method}\label{app:sub2}

	The proposed $\text{RSTP}$ method also demonstrates excellent control feasibility, as presented in Table~\ref{tab:main_exp}. Specifically, the M.TE of $\text{RSTP}$ shows a substantial reduction of \textbf{47.23\%} compared to BLM. This significant improvement indicates that the diffusion model can effectively and implicitly learn vehicle kinematics from the dataset. In contrast, methods such as $\text{A}^\star$, $\text{RRT}^\star$, and $\text{FMT}^\star$, which neglect vehicle kinematic constraints, lead to considerably larger tracking errors. Optimization-based methods, such as RITP and OBCA, achieve notable reductions in M.TE of 70.67\% and 79.27\%, respectively, yielding trajectories with strong control feasibility. However, this improved accuracy comes at a high computational cost. In comparison, $\text{RSTP}$ generates trajectories with strong control feasibility while demands lower computational resources.
	 \vspace{-10pt}

	\subsection{Effectiveness of Models Composition and Sensitivity Analysis of Compositional Weights}\label{app:comp_sim}


	The effectiveness of diffusion models composition for generalization is validated in scenes $CS_1$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:vis_abstract}) and $CS_2$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:exp_compose2}), employing four distinct compositional weight configurations, as summarized in Table~\ref{tab:comp_gen}. As indicated in the table, the composed model can safely generalize to unseen scenes when appropriate compositional weights are selected, achieving an F.Rate of \textbf{0\%}. However, increasing the weight assigned to conditional models results in a rise in F.Rate across both test scenes. These results demonstrate that the generalization capability is sensitive to compositional weights, and maintaining appropriate weights is critical for minimizing failures.






