% The results in 
Figure \ref{fig:colored_table} shows that the speaker embeddings primarily encode static spectral information (mean pitch, HNR, shimmer, $\alpha$-ratio) reflecting voice quality and frequency range.
On the other hand, the embeddings fail to capture dynamic behavioral identity markers like speech rate, voiced/unvoiced segment durations, variations in pitch, and loudness. This limitation means that embeddings represent only a %partial
subset of the speaker characteristics necessary for a comprehensive assessment of identity in speech synthesis.

While our goal is not to identify the best speaker embedding to use for identity measurement, we find that X-Vector embeddings encode a wider range of markers, including more dynamic information, than more recent and better-performing embeddings for the speaker verification task.
This agrees with the findings of \cite{fu2024asrrl}. This somewhat surprising result highlights the differences between speaker verification and speech synthesis, and calls for a more extensive comparison of ASV embeddings in the synthesis context.
Better-suited identity representations might even need to be developed.
% , or even the development of better-suited identity representations.
%Interestingly, when X-Vector is used on top of WavLM features, it no longer encodes dynamic information. \todo{This is because SSL models discard information during their training this trying to abstract low level information not necessarily useful the predict high level internal representation (see in the model implementation)}. 

Our experiment also shows that embeddings can encode distracting factors unrelated to identity, such as speech file duration. We now explore this limitation and its implications in more detail.

