\section{Introduction}
We are interested in the Navier-Stokes equations in $Q_T$
\begin{equation}\label{eq:NSE}
	\begin{cases}
		\begin{aligned}
			& \partial_t u + u\cdot \nabla u - \Delta u + \nabla p = 0 , \\
			& \operatorname{div}  u=0 , \\
			& u_{\vert_{t=-T}} = a(x), \quad \operatorname{div}  a(x)=0.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{cases}
\end{equation}

We assume that the initial data for the Navier-Stokes equations is smooth solenoidal vector fields in $\mathbb{R}^{3}$, decaying sufficiently fast as $|x| \rightarrow \infty$. There are many celebrated results on the Navier-Stokes equations. In particular, Leray in the seminal work in 1934 \cite{leray1934} introduced the concept of weak solutions to the Navier-Stokes equations,  relaxing the classical smoothness requirements for solutions. Leray proved the global existence of  weak solutions and the set of potential singularities must be confined to a sparse region. Afterwards, the regularity of weak solution of Navier-Stokes equations becomes one of the challenging open problems in mathematics. More precisely, whether the solution to \eqref{eq:NSE} can develop finite time singularities from the regular initial data remains a big question. And it has been called one of the seven most important unsolved problems in mathematics by the Clay Mathematics Institute.

A well-known scaling invariant property to \eqref{eq:NSE} is that for any given solution $(u,p)$ to \eqref{eq:NSE}, the scaled functions
$$
u_{\lambda}(x,t)=\lambda u(\lambda x, {\lambda}^2 t),\quad \quad p_{\lambda}(x,t)={\lambda}^2 p(\lambda x, {\lambda}^2 t)
$$
are also solutions to \eqref{eq:NSE} for any $\lambda >0$.

Leray established the following result:  Assume that $a(x)$ is a smooth solenoidal vector field on $\mathbb{R}^{3}$ decaying sufficiently fast as $|x| \rightarrow \infty$, then there exists a global Leray-Hopf weak solution $u(x,t)$. Moreover, these weak solutions also satisfy a global energy inequality. And the solution is actually a strong solution within a short time interval. Leray also established the blow-up rate of the $L^p$ norm of $u(x,t)$ when $p>3$.\\


Leray's results demonstrate that for the Navier-Stokes equations with sufficiently smooth initial data, the  global weak solution remains regular for a short time. However, it may develop singularities thereafter. The regularity theory for weak solutions based on the current known estimates for solutions are not enough, since those known a priori estimates for the Navier-Stokes equations are not good enough, in particular, even not scaling invariant.  A natural question for Navier-Stokes equations is whether solutions with sufficiently regular initial data remain smooth for all time or develop finite-time singularities.

	It is easy to see from the energy estimates,  Leray weak solutions are in the following space
	\begin{align}
		u(x,t) \in L^s(-T,0; L^l(\RR^3))   \quad \text{while} \quad \frac{3}{l}+\frac{2}{s}=\frac{3}{2}.
	\end{align}


	There are many works on the study of regularity of weak solution to the Navier-Stokes equations. We cannot review all those results but list a few related to our studies.
	One approach that developed is to focus on establishing regularity criteria. If the Leray-Hopf weak solution lies in a suitable scaling-invariant space, then the weak solution is actually smooth. One of the well-known results in this direction includes the following regularity theorems which are called Ladyzhenskaya-Prodi-Serrin condition.
	\begin{theorem}[Ladyzhenskaya-Prodi-Serrin]\label{LPScondition}
		Assume that $a(x)$ is a smooth solenoidal vector field on $\mathbb{R}^{3}$ and $a(x) \in  L^2(\RR^3)$. If $u(x,t)$ is a Leray-Hopf weak solution of Navier-Stokes equations\eqref{eq:NSE}, and in addition
		\begin{align}
		u(x,t) \in L^s(-T,t_0; L^l(\RR^3))   \quad \text{while} \quad \frac{3}{l}+\frac{2}{s}=1 ,\quad l\geq 3.
	\end{align}
		Then $u(x,t)$ is smooth in $\RR^3 \times [-T,t_0]$.
	\end{theorem}

	\begin{remark}
		This theorem was established by Prodi \cite{prodi1959}, Serrin \cite{Serrin1963}, Ladyzhenskaya \cite{Ladyzhenskaya1967} for $l>3$.
	\end{remark}

	\begin{remark}
		The critical case $s=\infty$, $ l=3$ was solved by  Escauriaza, Seregin and \v{S}ver\'{a}k in \cite{seregin2003} by a blow up argument together with a backward uniqueness result of the heat operator. It is worth noting that when $ l=3$, the regularity assumption loses a certain smallness property, while the backward uniqueness serves as a novel approach to overcome the lack of smallness.
	\end{remark}

	Subsequently, there are a series of works on regularity in scaling-invariant space such as  $L^\infty_t L^{3,\infty}_x$,  \, $ L^q_t \dot{B}^{-1+\frac{2}{q}}_{\infty,\infty} $ for $2<q<\infty$, (in \cite{1Seregin2021}; \cite{1CheskidovShvydkoy2010}). We cannot review all of them, but listed only a few them. And in \cite{1Tao2021}, through frequency decomposition Carelman inequality and  certain smallness property of high-frequency part, Tao obtained the blow-up rate of the $L^3$ norm. \\



	On the other hand, many researchers are interested in the investigation of the possible singularities. Although the general construction where the possible singularity occurs are still under investigation, some of the necessary conditions including the energy concentration conditions are established.  The partial regularity of solutions for Navier-Stokes equations are developed a series of small-energy regularity theorems, which were then utilized to estimate the  Hausdorff measure of singular point sets. This approach was initiated by Scheffer in a series of works \cite{1Scheffer1976},\cite{1Scheffer1977}. Then,  Caffarelli, Kohn, and Nirenberg in \cite{caffarelli1982} established a new small-energy regularity theorem, proved that the $1$-dimensional parabolic Hausdorff measure of the singular set of $u(x,t)$ is zero . Another important progress was made by Lin in \cite{lin1998},  where he introduced a blow up argument and provided a new short proof of the CKN small-energy regularity theorem.  Later Ladyzhenskaya-Seregin \cite{1LadyzhenskayaSeregin1999}, and Vasseur \cite{Vasseur2007} obtained alternative proofs of the CKN theorem.
	\begin{theorem}[CKN theorem]\label{CKN}
		Assume that  $\,(u(x,t)\,$,  $\,p(x,t))\,$ is a suitable weak solution of the Navier–Stokes equations in $Q(1)=B(1) \times (-1,0)$. There is a small constant $\varepsilon_0$, if
			\begin{equation} \label{regularity}
				\int_{Q(1)}|u(x, t)|^{3}+|p(x, t)|^{\frac{3}{2}} d x d t\leqslant \varepsilon_0.
			\end{equation}
			Then $u(x,t)$ is regular in $\overline{Q(\frac{1}{2})} $.

		As a result, the $1$-dimensional parabolic Hausdorff measure of the singular set of the solution is zero.
	\end{theorem}


	In this paper, we follow the regularity criteria approach mentioned above. Specifically, we prove that if a suitable weak solution  $\,(u(x,t), p(x,t))\, $ of the Navier-Stokes equations is in a scaling invariant space (which satisfies \eqref{pqcondition} and \eqref{pqcondition1}),   then  $\,u(x,t)\,$ is smooth.

Our proof employs the blow-up analysis and backward uniqueness techniques introduced by Escauriaza, Seregin and \v{S}ver\'{a}k in \cite{seregin2003}. While blow-up analysis was first introduced by Lin \cite{lin1998} for the study of Navier-Stokes equations in simplifying the CKN theorem. Then it is  well-established in the works of many authors,  such as, Escauriaza, Seregin and \v{S}ver\'{a}k in \cite{seregin2003},  Seregin  \cite{1Seregin2024} \cite{12Seregin2007}, Kikuchi and Seregin \cite{kikuchi2007}, Seregin and \v{S}ver\'{a}k \cite{1SereginSverak2009} \cite{1SereginSverak2017}, Rusin and \v{S}ver\'{a}k \cite{1RusinSverak2011},  Hao Jia and \v{S}ver\'{a}k  \cite{1JiaSverak2014},   Barker and Seregin \cite{1BarkerSeregin2017}, Ladyzhenskaya-Seregin \cite{1LadyzhenskayaSeregin1999}. Also,  Hao Jia and \v{S}ver\'{a}k in \cite{1JiaSverak2013} and Seregin in \cite{1Seregin2012}, both of these works overcame the lack of compactness via a blow-up argument. Under our assumptions, we need to pay attention to deal with the pressure and less decay in one space variable, due to the lack of regularity of the weak solutions.  In handling the pressure term, we adopted the pressure decomposition method developed by Lemarié-Rieusset in \cite{LemarieRieset2002}, which also used in \cite{1Seregin2012}.



	One of the difficulty arose in establishing the decay estimate at infinity, particularly due to the condition $ p_1 = +\infty$ , which fails to provide sufficient decay as  $x_3 \to \infty$ . As a result, the standard backward uniqueness argument used in \cite{seregin2003} is not directly applicable. However, we are able to prove that  $u(x,t)$  exhibits decay properties away from the $ x_3 $-axis, allowing us to apply backward uniqueness on a cone. For related studies on backward uniqueness in conical domains, we refer to \cite{1WuWang2015}, \cite{1LuLiSverak2012},  \cite{2WuZhang2016}, \cite{1LuLiSverak2012}.  \\

 It should be noted that in a recent work \cite{Lei2025}, Lei, Ren and Tian, under a geometric condition (which means that the directions of vorticity cannot avoid any great circle on the unit sphere),  they obtained a priori estimates for vorticity in a scaling invariant spaces and established a vorticity regularity criterion. More precisely when the vorticity $w(x,t)$ satisfies $w(x,t)  \in L_t^{\infty} L_{x_3}^{\infty} L_{x_h}^{1}$,  then they proved that the velocity $u(x,t)$ remains regular. In this work, we prove that if a suitable weak solutions $\,u(x,t)\,$ of the Navier-Stokes equations satisfies condition $\nabla u(x,t)  \in L_t^{\infty} L_{x_3}^{\infty} L_{x_h}^{1}$, then $u(x,t)$ is smooth.


	The main result of this paper is as follows.


		\subsection{Main result}

		We use the following notation
		$$
			\|f\|_{L_t^\infty L_{x_3}^{p_1} L_{x_h}^{p_2}(Q_T)} = \begin{cases}
						\underset{t \in( -T, 0) }{\text{ess sup} }(\int_{x_3}(\int_{x_h}|f|^{p_2} dx_h)^{\frac{p_1}{p_2}}d x_3)^{\frac{1}{p_1}} &2\leqslant  p_1 < \infty, \\
						\underset{\substack{t \in( -T, 0)\\ x_3 \in \RR}  }{\text{ess sup} } (\int_{x_h}|f|^{p_2} dx_h)^{\frac{1}{p_2}} & p_1 = \infty.
					\end{cases}
		$$

	\begin{theorem}\label{Main thm}
		Assume that  $v(x,t), p(x,t)$ is a suitable weak solution of the Navier-Stokes equations\eqref{eq:NSE}, if  $v(x,t), p(x,t)$ is smooth in $\RR^3 \times (-T,0)$, and $v(x,t)$ satisfies the additional regularity condition
		\begin{align}\label{pqcondition}
			\|v\|_{L_t^\infty L_{x_3}^{p_1} L_{x_h}^{p_2}(Q_T)}=M<\infty.
		\end{align}
		Where
		\begin{align}\label{pqcondition1}
			\frac{1}{p_1}+\frac{2}{p_2}=1, \quad \infty \geqslant p_1 \geqslant 2.
		\end{align}
		Then no singularity will form at $t=0$.
	\end{theorem}

	\begin{remark}
		The above theorem illustrates that if a suitable weak solution of the Navier-Stokes equations $v(x,t)$ satisfies condition \eqref{pqcondition} and \eqref{pqcondition1} in $\RR^3 \times (-T,0)$. Then $v(x,t)$ is regular in $\RR^3 \times (-T,0]$.
	\end{remark}
	\begin{remark}
		In condition \eqref{pqcondition}, the case $p_1=\infty , p_2=2$ presents difficulty since $v(x,t)$ lacks decay at spatial infinity in the $x_3$-direction.  The proof utilizes the backward uniqueness of the heat equation on conical regions, a property that holds when the opening angle of the cone is sufficiently large.
	\end{remark}
	\begin{remark}
		 By the observation  that  $\nabla v(x,t) \in L_t^{\infty} L_{x_3}^{\infty} L_{x_h}^{1}$ imply that $p_1=\infty \, \, p_2=2 $ in \eqref{pqcondition}. We have that when the velocity field $v(x,t)$ satisfies $\nabla v(x,t) \in L_t^{\infty} L_{x_3}^{\infty} L_{x_h}^{1}$, then $v(x,t)$ is regular.
	\end{remark}

	\subsection{Auxiliary lemmas}

	First we introduce the definition of a Leray-Hopf weak solution introduced in \cite{leray1934} and suitable weak solution introduced in \cite{caffarelli1982}.\\

	\textbf{Leray-Hopf weak solution:}  A Leray-Hopf weak solution of the Cauchy problem \eqref{eq:NSE} in $Q_{T}$ means a vector field $u(x,t): Q_{T} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^{3}$ satisfies the following conditions

	\begin{itemize}
        \item  Regularity
				\begin{align}
					u(x,t) \in L^{\infty}(-T,0; L^2(\RR^3)) \cap L^{2}\left(-T,0 ; W^{1,2}(\RR^3)\right).
				\end{align}
        \item  Weakly continuous
				\begin{align}
					\int_{\mathbb{R}^{3}} u(x, t_1) \cdot w(x) dx \rightarrow \int_{\mathbb{R}^{3}} u(x, t_2) \cdot w(x) dx \quad \text{as} \quad t_1 \rightarrow t_2 \quad \text{for any} \quad w(x) \in L^{2}(\mathbb{R}^{3};\RR^3).
	  			\end{align}
        \item 	The solution satisfies the Navier-Stokes equations in the sense of distribution
				\begin{align}
					\int_{Q_{T}}\left(-u(x,t) \cdot \partial_{t} w(x,t)-u(x,t) \otimes u(x,t): \nabla w(x,t)+\nabla u(x,t): \nabla w(x,t)\right) d x d t=0.
				\end{align}
				for all $w(x,t) \in C_{0,\sigma}^{\infty}\left(Q_{T}\right)$.  Where $C_{0,\sigma}^{\infty}\left(Q_{T}\right)$ means
				$$C_{0,\sigma}^{\infty}(\Omega_T)=\{w(x,t)\in C_{c}^{\infty}(\Omega_T;\mathbb{R}^3):\text{div}\,w(x,t) = 0\}.$$
        \item Energy inequality
				\begin{align}
					 \int_{\mathbb{R}^{3}}\left|u\left(x, t\right)\right|^{2} d x+2\int_{-T}^{t}\int_{\mathbb{R}^{3} }|\nabla u(x,t)|^{2} d x d t \leqslant \int_{\mathbb{R}^{3}}|a(x)|^{2} d x \quad \forall t \in[-T, 0].
				\end{align}
        \item The solution satisfies the initial value in the means of $L^2(\mathbb{R} ^3)$ norm
				\begin{align}
					\int_{\RR^3}|u(x,t)-a(x)|^2dx \rightarrow 0 \quad \text { as } \quad t \rightarrow -T.
				\end{align}
    \end{itemize}






	\textbf{Suitable Weak Solution:}  A suitable weak solution of the Navier-Stokes equations \eqref{eq:NSE} in $\Omega_T=\Omega \times(-T,0)$ is a pair of functions $u(x,t): \Omega_{T} \rightarrow \RR^{3}$ and $p(x,t): \Omega_{T} \rightarrow \RR$ satisfies the following conditions

	\begin{itemize}
        \item  Regularity
					\begin{align*}
					u(x,t) \in L^{\infty}(-T,0; L^2(\Omega)) \cap L^{2}\left(-T,0 ; W^{1,2}(\Omega)\right),
					\end{align*}
					\begin{align*}
						p(x,t) \in L^{\frac{3}{2}}(\Omega_T ).
					\end{align*}
        \item 	Satisfying the Navier-Stokes equations in the sense of distribution
				\begin{align}
					\int_{\Omega_{T}}\left(-u(x,t) \cdot \partial_{t} w(x,t)-u(x,t) \otimes u(x,t): \nabla w(x,t)+\nabla u(x,t): \nabla w(x,t)\right) d x d t=0.
				\end{align}
				for all $w(x,t) \in C_{0,\sigma}^{\infty}\left(\Omega_T \right)$.  Where $C_{0,\sigma}^{\infty}\left(\Omega_T \right)$ means
				$$C_{0,\sigma}^{\infty}(\Omega_T)=\{w(x,t)\in C_{c}^{\infty}(\Omega_T ;\mathbb{R}^3):\text{div}\,w(x,t) = 0\}.$$
        \item Local energy inequality
				For all non negative test function $\phi \in  C^\infty(\Omega_T)$ supported in $ K \times (-T,0)$,  where $K\Subset\Omega$. And for all $t \in (-T,0)$,
		\begin{equation}\label{local_e_i}
			\begin{split}
				&\int_{\Omega} |u(x,t)|^2 \phi(x,t) dx + 2\int_{-T}^{t}\int_{\Omega} |\nabla u(x,s)|^2 \phi(x,s) dx ds \\
				&\leq  \int_{-T}^{t}\int_{\Omega} |u(x,s)|^2 (\partial_s \phi(x,s) + \Delta \phi(x,s)) + u(x,s)\cdot\nabla\phi (|u(x,s)|^2 + 2p(x,s)) dx ds.
			\end{split}
		\end{equation}

    \end{itemize}


	In this work, we employ the $\varepsilon$-regularity theory established in \cite{lin1998} and \cite{gustafson2007}, which provides the following two lemmas.

		\begin{lemma}\label{elbsilon32}
			There is a small constant $\varepsilon_1$ , for any suitable weak solution $v(x,t), \, p(x,t)$ in $Q(z_0,1)=B(x_0,1)\times (t_0-1,t_0)$ . If
			\begin{equation}\label{small regularity}
				\lim_{r\to 0^+}\sup _{t_{0}-r^2 \leqslant t \leqslant t_{0}} \frac{1}{r^{\frac{3}{2}}} \int_{B\left(x_{0}, r\right)}|v(x, t)|^{\frac{3}{2}} d x\leqslant \varepsilon_1.
			\end{equation}
			Then $v(x,t)$ is regular at $z_0=(x_0,t_0)$.
		\end{lemma}

		\begin{lemma}\label{smallenergy-lin}
			There is a small constant $\varepsilon_0$ , for any suitable weak solution $v(x,t)\, , \, p(x,t)$ in $Q(z_0,1)$.  If
			\begin{equation}\label{regularity}
				\int_{Q\left(z_{0}, 1\right)}|v(x, t)|^{3}+|p(x, t)|^{\frac{3}{2}} d x d t\leqslant \varepsilon_0.
			\end{equation}
			then $v(x,t)$ is regular in $\overline{Q(z_0,\frac{1}{2})} .$
		\end{lemma}




	We will use the backward uniqueness of the heat equation on conical regions. Backward uniqueness over conical regions has been investigated in numerous works. We use the following Lemma from \cite{1WuWang2015}.
	\begin{lemma}\label{Backward-uniqueness}
	Assume  $\omega(x,t)$ defined on $\mathcal{C}_{\theta }  \times(0,T)$ ,  where  $\mathcal{C}_{\theta} = \left\{ x = (x_h, x_3) \,\big|\, x_h \in \mathbb{R}^2,\, x_3 > |x| \cos \frac{\theta}{2} \right\}$, satisfies the following properties
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $\omega$,  $\nabla\omega$, $\partial_{t}\omega$, $\nabla^{2}\omega$   $\in \,L^2(\mathcal{C}_{\theta }  \times(0,T))$;
        \item $|\partial_{t}\omega+\Delta\omega|\leq c(|\omega| + |\nabla\omega|)$ \label{eq:6.6.4} on  $\mathcal{C}_{\theta }  \times(0,T)$ for a positive constant $c$;
        \item $|\omega(x,t)|\leq M e^{M|x|^{2}}$ \label{eq:6.6.5} for all $x\in \mathcal{C}_{\theta }$,  $0 < t < T$, and for some $M>0$;
        \item $\omega(x,0) = 0$  for all $x\in \mathcal{C}_{\theta }$.
    \end{itemize}
    If $\theta \in (100.3^{\circ},180^{\circ}) $, then $\omega$ is identically zero in $\mathcal{C}_{\theta }\times(0,T)$.
	\end{lemma}


	The next lemma is the unique continuation of the heat operator (see \cite{seregin2003}).
	\begin{lemma}\label{unique-continuation}
	Assume $r,\, T>0$.   Suppose $w(x,t):B(r) \times (0,T)\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$ and $w, \partial_t w, \nabla w, \nabla^2 w \in L^2(B(r) \times (0,T))$, where $B(r)=\{x \in \RR^3:|x|<r\}$. \\

	\noindent If $w(x,t)$ satisfies that for some constant $c$
    \begin{equation*}
        |\partial_t w(x,t) + \Delta w(x,t)| \leq c(|w(x,t)| + |\nabla w(x,t)|) \quad \text{a.e. in } \quad B_r \times (0,T),
    \end{equation*}
    and
    \begin{equation*}
        w(x,t)(x^2 + t)^{-k} \in L^\infty(B(r) \times (0,T)), \quad \forall k\in\mathbb{N}.
    \end{equation*}
    then $w(x,0) = 0$ for all $x\in B(r/20)$.
	\end{lemma}




