\section{Numerical Test} \label{sec:Results}
\noindent
We perform numerical tests to validate the proposed method and to illustrate its potential. 
The majority of the physical parameters are selected within the realm of realistic biological systems (see Table \ref{tab:model_value}). Although some parameters are well-established, others, such as viscosity and surface tension of condensates, can exhibit significant variability, with observed ranges spanning four orders of magnitude. %\cite{wang2021surface}. 
Given that the aim of this paper is not to concentrate on a particular biological system, we intentionally vary parameters across a broad spectrum. Despite the numerical method's capability to handle phase-dependent viscosity \cite{aland2012benchmark}, we choose constant viscosity throughout this work for simplicity. 


\subsection{Validation} \label{sec:validation}
\noindent
% Basic parts of the present numerical model have been validated in Reference \cite{mokbel2021} and in Reference \cite{mokbel2020}. What distinguishes the present ALE method from that presented in \cite{mokbel2020} is the more implicit, and therefore significantly more robust, discretization of membrane forces. With the current model, it becomes feasible to couple partial differential equations (PDEs) defined on surfaces with those within the bulk, thus enabling the resolution of multi-dimensional systems of PDEs. 
% The model's stability and its capacity to tackle multi-dimensional problems render it distinctive and pioneering in the sense that it facilitates the investigation of phenomena hitherto unexplored through numerical simulations (as expounded in the subsequent sections).
% The validation of the ALE method presented in \cite{mokbel2020}  demonstrates that the simulations accurately represent membrane deformations arising from bending stiffness, in-plane stretching, and surface tension. 
% The current model attains an elevated level of accuracy due to the alteration in the calculation of the bending force, which contains a fourth-order derivative resolved implicitly through Finite Elements, integrated within the same system as the bulk equations. Consequently, it becomes feasible to substantially increase the time step sizes by up to a factor of 10,000 compared to the model from \cite{mokbel2020} fro bending dominant test cases without condensate droplets. This capability becomes particularly advantageous when employing a two phase fluid with adaptive grids around the membrane. Especially the usage of finer grids near the phase-field interface enables the simulations within this chapter that were unattainable with the previous methods.
% Reference \cite{mokbel2021} similarly employed the ALE method from \cite{mokbel2020}, yet it coupled an elastic solid with a two-phase fluid. The ALE method has also been applied to study the mechanical behaviour of cells in a fluidic channel \cite{Mokbel} and the self-propagation of elastic microswimmers \cite{mokbel2023}. The underlying flow solver has undergone validations against other numerical codes \cite{aland2012benchmark} and experimental data \cite{Aland2013,aland2014time}, including convergence studies \cite{aland2012benchmark, mokbel2020}. In addition, the numerical model from \cite{mokbel2021} was also validated for dynamic situations and applied to scenarios of moving contact lines \cite{MokbelStickSlip}. Consequently, the present model not only accurately represents stationary shapes but also exhibits precise capability in capturing dynamic phenomena.
To validate the numerical method, we consider membrane remodeling induced by a single droplet in 2D. Assuming an initially flat membrane sheet, which is significantly larger than the droplet, enables the analytical derivation of the stationary morphology based on shape equations (see Supplementary Material Appendix III). % (\autoref{sec:shapeEquations})). 
To compare with these analytical shapes, we setup the numerical method to simulate the evolution up to the stationary state. 
The membrane ends are connected to the boundary of the domain with a free-slip condition. Membrane stretching and permeability are neglected ($K_A=P=0$).

\autoref{fig:validation} shows the comparison of stationary shapes between numerical and analytical results.
The given local contact angle $\theta$ 
%can in the analytical case be computed by Young's relation
is related to the surface tensions through the Young-Dupré-law,
\begin{align}
    \cos\theta = \frac{\sigma_0-\sigma_1}{\sigma_{\text{f}}} \, ,\label{eq:theta}
\end{align}
and differs from the apparent contact angle $\alpha$ (see \autoref{fig:buddingSchematic}), which in the limit of both large droplets (compared to the capillarity length) and large vesicles (compared to the droplet) is 
given by Neumann's law \cite{kusumaatmaja2011}:
\begin{align}
    \cos\alpha = \frac{\sigma_0^2-\sigma_1^2-\sigma_{\text{f}}^2}{2\sigma_{\text{f}}\sigma_1}.
    \label{eq: angle alpha Neumann}
\end{align}
 % For comparability, both models are set up in 2D and the membrane is considered as a straight line (initially lying on the $x$-axis) in order to mimic an infinitely large spherical membrane in both methods. In the numerical method, 

The surface tensions have been chosen s.t. different resulting shapes can be compared, from a nearly spherical droplet with very low bending of the membrane \autoref{fig:validation}(a) over strong indentation of the membrane by the droplet \autoref{fig:validation}(d) to a lens-like shape of the droplet on the membrane \autoref{fig:validation}(f). For all different parameter configurations chosen, we observe a perfect agreement between numerical results and the results of the shape equations. 
This verifies that the numerical method accurately incorporates all surface forces and resolves the locally high membrane curvature in the triple contact point by the adaptive grid. 

The hydrodynamics of the numerical method and the underlying flow solver have been previously validated in various applications on cells and shells in fluid flow \cite{Mokbel,mokbel2023}. Furthermore, benchmark validations against other numerical codes \cite{aland2012benchmark}, experimental data \cite{Aland2013,aland2014time}, and convergence studies \cite{aland2012benchmark, mokbel2020} have been successfully conducted. 
In addition, the wetting boundary conditions were validated for dynamic situations and applied to scenarios of moving contact lines \cite{MokbelStickSlip}. 
Consequently, the method exhibits precise capabilities in capturing dynamic phenomena, which strongly suggest that it is well-suited for application in future studies leveraging realistic experimental data. 

