\subsection{Can existing intent classifiers detect impact-revealing citations?}
\label{sec:intentexternalmethods}

\noindent
\textbf{Comparison with existing methods. } We compare our impact-revealing classifier against the following popular intent classification methods: (i) Meaningful Citations~\cite{valenzuela2015identifying}: a supervised machine learning method to classify citation intents into meaningful or non-meaningful, by leveraging context and citation metadata;  (ii) Structural Scaffolds~\cite{cohan-etal-2019-structural}: a multi-task model which enhances the main task by integrating auxiliary tasks; (iii)  Multi-cite~\cite{lauscher2022multicite}: a multi-label classification method that predicts multiple intents using a fine-tuned   SciBERT~\cite{beltagy2019scibert}. We map their intent classes to either ``impact-revealing'' or ``other''. More details are provided in Appendix~\ref{externalsetup}. For our method, we use the ICL prompt, with K=50 and LLM=GPT4o-mini. For every test instance, we run our prompt 3 times (shuffling the order of the shots), and take a majority vote for the classification. We note that the results of the 3 runs have a full agreement of 72\%. 







\noindent
\textbf{Results. } Numerical results are reported in Table~\ref{intent_classifier_external_methods}. Our method demonstrates the best performance on this task, with the most significant improvement in recall, outperforming the second-best method by 19 percentage points. This is particularly crucial for our goal in generating impact summaries from impact-revealing citations because the highest recall in this context means that our method ensures that fewer meaningful citations are missed. The qualitative results are shown in the appendix~\ref{externalsetup}.





\subsection{Ablation study for impact summary generation}
\label{sec:ablation}


\noindent
\textbf{Data: papers, citations, intents.} We select 105 papers from the Semantic Scholar Academic Graph (S2AG)\footnote{\url{https://www.semanticscholar.org/product/api}}, from psychology, medicine and computer science (35 each), published between 1974 and 2022 at top venues in their respective fields. The citation count for each article ranges from $\sim$ 500 to $\sim$ 5000. We collect citations and their contexts using the citation lookup feature in the S2AG API. We first run our fine-grained intent generator (with K=50) and classifier for every citation context. This amounts to 70k citation contexts with their generated intents and their classes.  


\noindent
\textbf{Prompt variants.}  We experiment with different input configurations to assess their effect on the summary's quality, focusing on: (1) Citation contexts, including all, only impact-revealing, or none, to test what the LLM infers without citations and how varying context types affect the summary. Although full citation contexts can enrich summaries, they can also introduce noise from incidental references~\cite{Liu2023LostIT}. (2) Fine-grained intents, whether to include the intents alongside citation contexts. 

Table~\ref{table:ablation_results} lists all variants. In total, we generate 945 impact summaries\footnote{1 variant without citations + (4 citation-based variants * 2 orderings) * 105 papers}. See Appendix~\ref{app:generateprompt} for the prompt and schema. For the intent generation task, we choose GPT-4o-mini due to its lower cost. For both impact generation and evaluation, the more advanced tasks, we use GPT-4o.





\noindent
\textbf{Results. } Table~\ref{table:ablation_results} shows the results for all metrics. Even though it shows a good understanding of how the paper was cited, likely due to the LLM's training data having access to a large corpus of scholarly articles, the zero-shot variant is the least faithful, and we observe that prompts with only impact-revealing citations generate more faithful impact summaries. This suggests that longer contexts might induce hallucinations in the generation process. Interestingly, providing the citation intents also gives a slight boost to the faithfulness of the impact summaries. A possible reason is that the intents encourage wording that aligns with the input paper's citations. 

Coverage is especially challenging, requiring a balance of completeness, relevance, and conciseness. Still, our variant outperforms the baseline by 9\%, showing the LLM can capture key themes. Some inputs contain over 100 distinct themes, explaining modest overall scores. When evaluating only the top-3 most frequent themes (the largest 3 clusters), coverage rises significantly, as our best variant reaches 0.75 (+17\%), highlighting its ability to capture the most important aspects of a paper's impact. Variants exposed only to impact-revealing citations show higher citation year compliance, while adding citation intents lowers it, likely because models focus more on intent than timing. Examples are in Appendix~\ref{app:ablationexamples}, with further discussion in Section~\ref{sec:limit}. 

All variants score high on trend awareness, with a slight edge for the impact-revealing + intents variant, showing LLMs can recognize distinct impact periods. This variant also improves insightfulness and specificity by 13\% over the zero-shot baseline. Qualitative examples are in Appendix~\ref{app:ablationexamples}. G-Eval, via DeepEval\footnote{\url{https://github.com/confident-ai/deepeval}}, also offers reasons for each score (examples in Appendix~\ref{app:gevalexamples}). We visualize sample impact summaries in Appendix~\ref{app:impactexamples}. To examine whether results change depending on the field of the paper, we report results per field in Appendix~\ref{app:resultsperfield}. 
%Coverage is arguably the most challenging metric, as impact summaries must strike a balance between completeness, relevance, and conciseness. Despite these challenges, our variant outperforms the baseline in coverage by 9 \%. This indicates that the LLM can effectively capture some of the key themes present in the input context. It is worth noting that, in some cases, over a hundred distinct themes were identified in the citation context, which helps explain why the overall coverage scores remain relatively modest. To better understand the model’s performance, we also evaluate coverage with respect to the top-3 most frequent themes in the input citation context. Under this constraint, the scores improve significantly, as our best variant achieves a coverage of 0.75 (+17\% over baseline), showing its ability to capture the most important mentioned elements of how a paper has been utilized. 


%The citation year compliance of variants exposed solely to impact-revealing citations is higher. Providing citation intents, on the other hand, has the opposite effect, and variants given this information achieve lower citation year compliance scores. We hypothesize that the models assign evidence based on the citation intents in such cases, focusing less on citation year compliance. In Appendix~\ref{app:ablationexamples}, we show examples of evaluating these metrics. We further discuss the relatively low results of year compliance and coverage more in Section~\ref{sec:limit}. For informativeness, we observe very high scores of trend awareness by all variants, with a slight advantage for our the impact-revealing with intents variant, indicating the ability of LLMs to recognize different impact time periods with distinctive titles. For insightfulness and specificity, again, the same variant shows an improvement of 13\%, on both metrics, over the zero-shot baseline. We show qualitative examples in Appendix~\ref{app:ablationexamples}.  On top of numerical scores, G-Eval's implementation in the DeepEval framework\footnote{\url{https://github.com/confident-ai/deepeval}} offers a reason for every returned numerical score. We show a few examples of returned reasons in Table~\ref{app:gevalexamples}. We visualize the impact statements for selected papers in Appendix~\ref{app:impactexamples}.





\textbf{LLM-Human correlation} We compute F1 agreement between human and LLM evaluators (50 instances per metric), yielding scores of 0.83 for faithfulness, 0.71 for coverage, 0.66 for insightfulness, 0.86 for trend awareness, and 0.74 for specificity. Correlation metrics (Spearman and Kendall-Tau) in Appendix~\ref{app:humancorrelation} show moderate to strong statistical significance.


