The walking procedure takes $\approx44\%$ of the execution time on the GPU. In contrast, random-number generation takes the majority ($\approx 72\%$) of the time in the Metropolis algorithm. Table~\ref{tab:perf} shows the time required for the execution of the two main kernels of the standard Metropolis algorithm and the new algorithm proposed for a lattice with $160^3$ spins and 128 replicas using a single walker. Using more walkers, the spin-update time increases linearly as expected (data not reported). By summing the timings for the two kernels of the new algorithm and taking into account the total number of spins, we see that the spin-update time is $\approx 0.4$ picoseconds. The test was executed on an Nvidia H100 GPU. We argue that the higher the peak performance of the platform, the greater the advantage provided by minimizing the use of random numbers. In particular, the design of an ASIC would be greatly simplified under the assumption that generating random numbers is not an issue.

